 22:  *
 23:  * What goes out there is read off each border tile, not off the map. A map is
 24:  * not one thing all the way round: Lorencia's north border is 98% water tiles
 25:  * and its west border is 13% - sea on one side, forest on the other - and a
 26:  * map-wide choice gets one of them wrong. A border tile that is water carries
 27:  * its sea out at its own level, with the map's own water tile scrolling in
 28:  * step; every other tile carries its own ground out. Where the two meet, an
 29:  * apron drops the shore into the water.
 30:  *
 31:  * Outward it is rings, not one long quad (§ `RING_DEPTHS`): a single quad per
 32:  * border tile extrudes that tile's art three hundred tiles into the distance,
 33:  * and the border row's every step - a road, a shadow, a patch of sand - comes
 34:  * out as a stripe converging on the horizon. The rings let the fill leave the
 35:  * map as an exact copy of the tile it grew from, shuffle its art as it goes,
 36:  * and settle into one open field by the time the haze has it.
 37:  *
 38:  * It shares the ground's `ShaderMaterial` instance rather than owning one.
 39:  * That is the whole trick: the tile array, the water scroll, the bake in the
 40:  * vertex colour, the sky and ground light and the haze all reach the frame
 41:  * with no new shader code, and there is no seam at the boundary because both
 42:  * sides are the same program with the same uniforms.
 43:  *
 44:  * A separate mesh, though, not extra rows in the ground: the ground is
 45:  * ray-cast for click-to-move several times a second and carries a submesh
 46:  * octree, and geometry reaching hundreds of tiles out would widen both.
 47:  */
