 442:    */
 443:   // Back up from 1.1: that was tuned while the field clipped to white, where
 444:   // any relief read as ripple. With the field at 0.86 the drifts need real
 445:   // slope to show volume - the reference banks visibly against walls.
 446:   relief: 0.55, // was 2.2: the field banded into blurred stripes; improved_2.jpg is near-flat (trench has TRAIL_GAIN)
 447:   /**
 448:    * How far the troughs between drifts go toward skylight, 0..1.
 449:    *
 450:    * A tint rather than a darkening: what a trough loses is the warm direct
 451:    * sun, and what is left is the cold half of the sky. Runs after the shading
 452:    * floor, so the darkest place on a snowfield is a cool blue rather than a
 453:    * black hole - the same complaint the footprint work ran into, fixed the
 454:    * same way and with the same colour so the two agree on one map.
 455:    */
 456:   cavity: 0.35,
 457:   /**
 458:    * Sheen off the lit faces, plus the sparse glints. Snow is not matte, but
 459:    * the reference is close to it: a soft broad sheen and rare glints, no
 460:    * ripple highlights. 0.3 with the old relief put a highlight on every
 461:    * ripple crest; at the new relief 0.2 is a broad sheen only.
 462:    */
 463:   sheen: 0.08, // reference snow is matte
 464:   /**
 465:    * Crystal grain: the texture of the surface itself, under the drifts.
 466:    *
 467:    * Not the same thing as the mottling that was cut two rounds ago, and the
 468:    * difference is the scale. That was a feature about a TILE across, which the
 469:    * eye reads as noise laid over the ground. This is nine repeats per tile -
 470:    * well under a footprint - which the eye reads as what the ground is MADE
 471:    * OF. Without it a fully covered field is a smooth wash with some large soft
 472:    * shading on it, and it looks smudged however good the drifts are.
 473:    *
 474:    * Small, and it has to be. The grain folds into the same normal as the
 475:    * drifts, so too much of it does not sit on top of the structure - it
 476:    * REPLACES it. Measured as detail energy in two spatial bands, sub-tile
 477:    * against drift-scale:
 478:    *
 479:    *     0.008   2.56x fine   1.03x coarse
 480:    *     0.015   4.50x fine   1.11x coarse   <- chosen
 481:    *     0.022   6.45x fine   1.22x coarse
 482:    *     0.05   13.44x fine   1.78x coarse
 483:    *
 484:    * 0.05 buys five times the surface texture for a fifth of the drift
 485:    * structure, which is the trade worth making.
 486:    */
 487:   // Halved from 0.015 against the reference frames: with post-processing
 488:   // off there is no bloom to soften it, and 0.015 read as a wind-ripple
 489:   // texture over the whole field. The reference surface is smooth with a
 490:   // faint grain only visible near the camera.
 491:   grain: 0.003,
 492:   /**
 493:    * Pushes every drift deeper without moving where snow settles. Separate from
 494:    * coverage, which decides *whether* there is snow here; this is how much.
 495:    */
 496:   depth: 0,
 497: };
