 937:  * ice showing through, which is the opposite of what a fire just did. The
 938:  * same multiply the rain uses says "this ground is wet" in the one vocabulary
 939:  * the map already has.
 940:  */
 941: const MELT_DAMP: readonly [number, number, number] = [0.63, 0.65, 0.7];
 942: 
 943: /**
 944:  * How hard the sun term swings the albedo either side of 1.
 945:  *
 946:  * Deliberately not a second dial. The shading is meant to follow the geometry
 947:  * - a steeper drift catches more light because its normal says so - so this
 948:  * stays fixed and `SNOW_SHADE.relief` moves the slope that feeds it. Two
 949:  * knobs would let the shading and the shape disagree with each other.
 950:  */
 951: const LIT_GAIN = 1.0;
 952: 
 953: /**
 954:  * The least of its own albedo a drift's shaded face may keep.
 955:  *
 956:  * Two jobs, and the first is not cosmetic: without it the multiplier goes
 957:  * **negative** on a face steep enough and turned far enough from the sun, and
 958:  * a negative albedo is not a dark drift - it is whatever the rest of the
 959:  * pipeline happens to do with a negative colour. A sweep found this at a
 960:  * relief of 1.3 and a gain of 2.2, both inside the range the console hook
 961:  * lets someone dial to.
 962:  *
 963:  * The second is the note from the footprint work: a snow shadow is never
 964:  * black. It is snow lit by the sky instead of the sun, which is why the
 965:  * cavity tint runs after this and why the floor sits well above zero. 0.45
 966:  * landed the darkest face at a little under half the lit one, and with
 967:  * post-processing off that is the dark blue field in the "before" shot;
 968:  * the reference's deepest drift shade measures ~0.72 of the lit snow.
 969:  */
 970: const SHADE_FLOOR = 0.55;
 971: 
 972: /**
 973:  * What survives in the troughs: skylight, once the warm direct sun is gone.
 974:  *
 975:  * Devias' own blue, not a neutral grey. The map's shadows read blue-violet
 976:  * (snow under an overcast sky); a desaturated grey here was fighting that
 977:  * rather than joining it, and read as "dark" instead of as shade. This is
 978:  * hue 232 at half saturation - the same blue the map's own
 979:  * shadows are, so snow in shade matches stone in shade.
 980:  *
 981:  * Baked rather than exposed - it is gated by `SNOW_SHADE.cavity`, so it
 982:  * cannot fail into anything on its own.
 983:  */
 984: // Half the chroma of the 0.66/0.74/0.93 it was: the same hue at full
 985: // saturation reads as blue paint rather than blue-grey; `cavity` was raised
 986: // to keep the same amount of colour in the troughs.
 987: const CAVITY_TINT = 'vec3(0.72, 0.75, 0.83)';
 988: 
 989: /**
 990:  * Range over which surface detail fades to a flat wash, in world units - a
 991:  * tile is 1.
 992:  *
 993:  * Sized against the game's actual camera, not to taste. The ArcRotateCamera in
 994:  * `testScene` sits at a radius of 10, so ground under the player is about ten
 995:  * tiles away and the far edge of the view is somewhere near thirty. Working
 996:  * that through: the finer relief octave has a period of 0.38 tiles, which at
 997:  * thirty tiles and this obliquity still lands on several pixels, so the noise
 998:  * does not actually alias anywhere the player normally looks.
 999:  *
1000:  * Which makes this insurance rather than a fix, and it is deliberately set
1001:  * where insurance belongs - past everything on screen. A fade that started at
1002:  * sixteen (the first guess here) would have flattened the far half of every
1003:  * shot and put a visible falloff band across the middle of the ground. What
1004:  * it still covers is the pathological case: a wider screen, a zoomed-out
1005:  * view, or ground seen across the map, where the drift really does fall under
1006:  * a pixel and the gradient would turn into shimmer.
1007:  */
1008: /**
1009:  * Repeats of the crystal grain per world unit - a tile is 1.
1010:  *
1011:  * Nine, so a grain feature is about an eighth of a tile: comfortably under a
1012:  * footprint, which is the scale that reads as "this surface is made of
1013:  * something" rather than "someone laid noise over the ground".
1014:  */
1015: const GRAIN_SCALE = 12;
1016: 
1017: /**
1018:  * How much the ripple is stretched along the wind, as a frequency ratio.
1019:  *
1020:  * Both axes have to stay FINE. Stretching is what makes a ripple, but it also
1021:  * divides the frequency along the wind, and at GRAIN_SCALE 9 a stretch of 0.22
1022:  * put the long axis at 2 repeats per tile - a half-tile feature, which is
1023:  * exactly the mottling scale that was cut two rounds ago for reading as noise
1024:  * laid over the ground. The streaks in play were a tile long and smeared.
1025:  *
1026:  * Raising the scale and easing the stretch keeps the ripple and takes the long
1027:  * axis back under a print: 20 * 0.35 = 7 repeats per tile, a feature every
1028:  * 0.14 tiles, against 20 across at 0.05.
1029:  */
1030: const GRAIN_STRETCH = 0.35;
1031: 
1032: /**
1033:  * Roughness of the sheen lobe along the wind and across it.
1034:  *
1035:  * Both far broader than the pow(NdotH, 42) they replaced - that exponent is
1036:  * about a 0.15 lobe, isotropic, which is a wet surface and not a dry one. Snow
1037:  * is rough; what makes it read as snow rather than as ice is that the
1038:  * highlight is a smeared band along the ripples, not a point.
1039:  */
1040: const SHEEN_ALONG = 0.62;
1041: const SHEEN_ACROSS = 0.2;
1042: 
1043: /** Glitter cells per world unit, and how few of them fire. */
1044: const GLITTER_SCALE = 150;
1045: const GLITTER_RARITY = 0.978;
1046: 
1047: /** How bright a firing crystal is against the sheen it sits on. */
1048: const GLITTER_GAIN = 3;
1049: 
1050: /** The wind's axis. Deliberately off both world axes so it cannot read as the tile grid. */
1051: const WIND_COS = 0.819;
1052: const WIND_SIN = 0.574;
1053: 
1054: /** Finite-difference step for the grain. Its own, matched to its own period. */
1055: const GRAIN_STEP = 0.014;
1056: 
1057: /**
1058:  * Range over which the grain fades out, in world units.
1059:  *
1060:  * Much shorter than the drifts' fade, and for the reason that fade exists at
1061:  * all: a 0.11-tile feature falls under a pixel far closer to the camera than a
1062:  * 3-tile one does, and past that point it contributes nothing but shimmer.
1063:  */
1064: const GRAIN_NEAR = 10;
1065: const GRAIN_FAR = 26;
1066: 
1067: const DETAIL_NEAR = 30;
1068: const DETAIL_FAR = 55;
1069: 
1070: /**
1071:  * The fragment-shader body. Returns '' when the map has no layers, so the
1072:  * unchanged shader is genuinely unchanged.
1073:  *
1074:  * `finalColorVar` is the albedo the caller has already resolved from the splat
1075:  * layers; each layer is applied to it in place, in list order.
1076:  *
1077:  * `openVar` is a float already in scope: 1 for open sky, 0 for roofed.
1078:  */
1079: export function terrainOverlayGlsl(
1080:   overlays: readonly TerrainOverlay[],
1081:   finalColorVar: string,
1082:   openVar: string
1083: ): string {
1084:   if (overlays.length === 0) return '';
1085: 
1086:   const blocks = overlays.map((o, i) => {
1087:     const c = o.colour;
1088:     const blended =
1089:       o.blend === 'multiply'
1090:         ? `${finalColorVar}.rgb * vec3(${f(c[0])}, ${f(c[1])}, ${f(c[2])})`
1091:         : `vec3(${f(c[0])}, ${f(c[1])}, ${f(c[2])})`;
1092: 
1093:     // Art the layer defers to: its colour becomes the ground's own wherever
1094:     // the ground is already the substance being drawn. Before the surface
1095:     // terms, so the drifts and the grain shape the map's texture there
1096:     // instead of shaping a flat colour laid over it - the layer keeps its
1097:     // depth, it just stops repainting what is already snow.
1098:     const artGlsl = !o.defersToArt
1099:       ? ''
1100:       : `
1101:       float ovArt${i} = ovArtMatch(
1102:         ${finalColorVar}.rgb,
1103:         vec2(${f(o.defersToArt.lum[0])}, ${f(o.defersToArt.lum[1])}),
1104:         vec2(${f(o.defersToArt.chroma[0])}, ${f(o.defersToArt.chroma[1])}));
1105:       ovCol${i} = mix(ovCol${i}, ${finalColorVar}.rgb, ovArt${i});
1106: `;
1107: 
1108:     const grain = o.grain ?? 0;
1109:     const grainScale = o.grainScale ?? 1;
1110: 
1111:     // A relief layer takes its albedo wobble from the surface it already has,
1112:     // inside the relief block below. Two reasons: the separate grain noise was
1113:     // value noise, so it was contributing its own quilting to the thing this
1114:     // change exists to fix; and a snowfield's bright and dull patches are the
1115:     // drifts, not an unrelated field laid over them.
1116:     const grainGlsl =
1117:       grain > 0 && !o.relief
1118:         ? `
1119:       // Surface of the layer itself: two octaves of the same noise, as a
1120:       // brightness wobble either side of 1. Drifts, not patches.
1121:       float ovG${i} =
1122:           overlayNoise(vWorldXZ * ${f(grainScale)}) * 0.6 +
1123:           overlayNoise(vWorldXZ * ${f(grainScale * 3.1)}) * 0.4;
1124:       ovCol${i} *= 1.0 + ${f(grain)} * (ovG${i} - 0.5) * 2.0;
1125: `
1126:         : '';
1127: 
1128:     // The surface of the layer, and every term that hangs off it. Emitted
1129:     // only for a layer that declares relief, so the wet-weather layers keep
1130:     // the exact shader they had.
1131:     const reliefGlsl = !o.relief
1132:       ? ''
1133:       : `
1134:       // ---- the layer's own surface ---------------------------------------
1135:       //
1136:       // Depth, which is not ovAmount. Amount saturates once the ground has
1137:       // gone white; depth keeps climbing past that, and the gap between the
1138:       // two is the difference between "the ground is white here" and "there
1139:       // is a drift here". Deepest where the break-up noise is low, which is
1140:       // where snow collects.
1141:       float ovDepth${i} = ovAmount${i} * clamp(
1142:         0.35 + (ovTarget${i} - ovBreakup${i}) + ovShade.w, 0.0, 1.0);
1143: 
1144:       float ovHc${i} = ovRelief${i}(vWorldXZ);
1145: 
1146:       // Albedo wobble, off the surface rather than off a field of its own.
1147:       ovCol${i} *= 1.0 + ${f(o.grain ?? 0)} * (ovHc${i} - 0.5) * 2.0;
1148:       float ovHx${i} = ovRelief${i}(vWorldXZ + vec2(${f(RELIEF_STEP)}, 0.0));
1149:       float ovHz${i} = ovRelief${i}(vWorldXZ + vec2(0.0, ${f(RELIEF_STEP)}));
1150: 
1151:       // Gradient of that field, as a surface normal, blended toward the
1152:       // ground's own by depth: a dusting follows the terrain, a drift has a
1153:       // shape of its own.
1154:       //
1155:       // The blend treats the bump as if the ground were level and so
1156:       // under-rotates on a slope. Affordable because slopeKnee fades the
1157:       // layer out well before the ground is steep enough for that to show.
1158:       // Procedural noise has no mip chain, so past the distance at which a
1159:       // drift is narrower than a pixel the gradient stops being a surface and
1160:       // becomes shimmer. Fade the *detail* out with range while leaving depth
1161:       // alone, because depth also decides coverage and distant snow should
1162:       // still be snow - just flat snow, which is what it was before this
1163:       // existed and is exactly what it should degrade to.
1164:       //
1165:       // This also carries the failure mode for cameraPosition: unbound it
1166:       // reads as the origin, the distance becomes the fragment's own distance
1167:       // from world zero, and every terrain fragment on a 256-tile map lands
1168:       // past the far end of the fade. An unbound uniform switches the feature
1169:       // off rather than lighting the map from an arbitrary place.
1170:       vec3 ovEye${i} = cameraPosition - vWorldPos;
1171:       float ovDetail${i} = ovDepth${i} *
1172:         (1.0 - smoothstep(${f(DETAIL_NEAR)}, ${f(DETAIL_FAR)}, length(ovEye${i})));
1173: 
1174:       float ovGain${i} = ovShade.x * ${f(o.relief)} / ${f(RELIEF_STEP)};
1175: 
1176:       // Crystal grain, on its own step because it is on its own scale. Fades
1177:       // out far sooner than the drifts do: a 0.11-tile feature falls under a
1178:       // pixel much closer to the camera than a 3-tile one, and past that point
1179:       // it stops being texture and starts being shimmer.
1180:       float ovGc${i} = ovGrainAt${i}(vWorldXZ);
1181:       float ovGx${i} = ovGrainAt${i}(vWorldXZ + vec2(${f(GRAIN_STEP)}, 0.0));
1182:       float ovGz${i} = ovGrainAt${i}(vWorldXZ + vec2(0.0, ${f(GRAIN_STEP)}));
1183:       float ovGg${i} = ovGrain * ovDepth${i} / ${f(GRAIN_STEP)} *
1184:         (1.0 - smoothstep(${f(GRAIN_NEAR)}, ${f(GRAIN_FAR)}, length(ovEye${i})));
1185: 
1186:       // The ploughed trail: its own gradient step, because a rim is far
1187:       // finer than a drift, scaled by the drift gain so the channel walls
1188:       // are lit by exactly the rule the drift faces are. Gated on amount,
1189:       // not detail - a trail is a shape in the snow at any distance.
1190:       float ovTc${i} = 0.0;
1191:       float ovTgx${i} = 0.0;
1192:       float ovTgz${i} = 0.0;
1193:       float ovTd${i} = 0.0;
1194: ${
1195:         o.trail
1196:           ? `
1197:       ovTd${i} = ovTrailAt${i}(vWorldXZ) * ovAmount${i};
1198:       if (ovTd${i} > 0.0) {
1199:         ovTc${i} = ovTrailShape${i}(vWorldXZ);
1200:         float ovTg${i} = ${f(TRAIL_GAIN)} / ${f(TRAIL_STEP)} * ovAmount${i};
1201:         ovTgx${i} = (ovTrailShape${i}(vWorldXZ + vec2(${f(TRAIL_STEP)}, 0.0)) - ovTc${i}) * ovTg${i};
1202:         ovTgz${i} = (ovTrailShape${i}(vWorldXZ + vec2(0.0, ${f(TRAIL_STEP)})) - ovTc${i}) * ovTg${i};
1203:       }`
1204:           : ''
1205:       }
1206: 
1207:       vec3 ovBump${i} = normalize(vec3(
1208:         -(ovHx${i} - ovHc${i}) * ovGain${i} - (ovGx${i} - ovGc${i}) * ovGg${i} - ovTgx${i},
1209:         1.0,
1210:         -(ovHz${i} - ovHc${i}) * ovGain${i} - (ovGz${i} - ovGc${i}) * ovGg${i} - ovTgz${i}
1211:       ));
1212:       // The snow's own normal, built WITHOUT reference to vNormal.
1213:       //
1214:       // The mix that used to be here dragged the terrain's normal into the
1215:       // snow's lighting, and that normal is flat per tile: the ground is a
1216:       // quad soup with four unshared vertices each, so every tile is a facet.
1217:       // Under a texture nobody notices; under a smooth white layer the facets
1218:       // become the dominant structure and the snowfield reads as a grid of
1219:       // slightly different squares - a large part of what kept coming back as
1220:       // "pixelated". The bake still carries the terrain's real shape, which is
1221:       // where that belongs.
1222:       float ovNmix${i} = max(ovDetail${i}, ovTd${i});
1223:       vec3 ovN${i} = normalize(vec3(
1224:         ovBump${i}.x * ovNmix${i},
1225:         1.0,
1226:         ovBump${i}.z * ovNmix${i}
1227:       ));
1228: 
1229:       // Sun on that normal, folded into the albedo rather than added after
1230:       // the fact, so the drift is still cut by the cascades and still coloured
1231:       // by the map's own bake - a drift in the shade of a wall is a shaded
1232:       // drift. That is the whole reason this belongs in the terrain shader and
1233:       // not in a decal.
1234:       //
1235:       // What shades is the DEVIATION from the ground's own normal, not the
1236:       // absolute dot product. Two reasons, and the first one is why the drifts
1237:       // did not show up at all on the first attempt:
1238:       //
1239:       //  - On near-level ground dot(vNormal, sun) is about 0.81 and the
1240:       //    perturbation is worth about 0.09 either side of it. An absolute
1241:       //    term therefore spends nine tenths of its range on a constant and
1242:       //    one tenth on the shape it exists to show. Centring it hands the
1243:       //    whole range to the shape.
1244:       //  - The constant part is a flat brightening of every snow fragment,
1245:       //    which is the bake's job and not this term's. Worse, it disappears
1246:       //    at the far end of the detail fade, so distant snow would read
1247:       //    dimmer than near snow with a visible band between them.
1248:       // Centred on level ground rather than on vNormal, for the same reason.
1249:       float ovNdl${i} = dot(ovN${i}, ovSunDir) - ovSunDir.y;
1250:       ovCol${i} *= max(
1251:         1.0 + ${f(LIT_GAIN)} * ovNdl${i} * ovSunOn * ovNmix${i},
1252:         ${f(SHADE_FLOOR)});
1253: 
1254:       // Troughs between drifts lose sky. What they lose is the warm direct
1255:       // sun, so what is left is cooler than the lit face - a tint rather than
1256:       // a plain darkening, which is the same reasoning as the footprints'
1257:       // cavity colour and keeps the two agreeing on one map.
1258:       // Only the genuine troughs. The band used to open at 0.2, which is most
1259:       // of a field whose height runs 0..1 - so the tint was washing over
1260:       // roughly half the snow instead of sitting in the hollows of it.
1261:       float ovCav${i} = max(
1262:         (1.0 - smoothstep(0.42, 0.95, ovHc${i})) * ovDetail${i},
1263:         smoothstep(0.2, 0.8, ovTd${i}));
1264:       // The channel floor sees less sky than any trough between drifts.
1265:       ovCol${i} *= 1.0 - ${f(TRAIL_AO)} * smoothstep(0.3, 1.0, ovTd${i});
1266:       ovCol${i} *= mix(
1267:         vec3(1.0), ${CAVITY_TINT}, clamp(ovShade.y * ovCav${i}, 0.0, 1.0));
1268: 
1269:       // Sheen, in two parts, because snow scatters at two scales at once.
1270:       //
1271:       // The first version of this was a single tight pow(NdotH, 42), and
1272:       // that is precisely the "polished glass" read: an isotropic lobe that
1273:       // narrow puts ONE sharp highlight on a surface which in reality throws
1274:       // light along its ripples. Snow is rough and directional, and the lobe
1275:       // has to be too.
1276:       vec3 ovView${i} = normalize(ovEye${i});
1277:       vec3 ovHalf${i} = normalize(ovView${i} + ovSunDir);
1278: 
1279:       // Ward-style anisotropic lobe about the wind axis: broad ALONG the
1280:       // ripple, tight across it. Same axis the grain is stretched on, so the
1281:       // sheen elongates in the direction the surface actually runs.
1282:       vec3 ovWindT${i} = normalize(vec3(${f(WIND_COS)}, 0.0, ${f(WIND_SIN)}));
1283:       vec3 ovWindB${i} = normalize(cross(ovN${i}, ovWindT${i}));
1284:       float ovHt${i} = dot(ovHalf${i}, ovWindT${i});
1285:       float ovHb${i} = dot(ovHalf${i}, ovWindB${i});
1286:       float ovHn${i} = max(dot(ovHalf${i}, ovN${i}), 0.0001);
1287:       float ovWard${i} = exp(-(
1288:         ovHt${i} * ovHt${i} / ${f(SHEEN_ALONG * SHEEN_ALONG)} +
1289:         ovHb${i} * ovHb${i} / ${f(SHEEN_ACROSS * SHEEN_ACROSS)}
1290:       ) / (ovHn${i} * ovHn${i}));
1291: 
1292:       // Glitter: individual crystals catching the sun, and the reason they
1293:       // twinkle is that each one is tilted its own way. A sparse hash picks
1294:       // which cells fire and a second pair gives each its OWN micro-normal, so
1295:       // a glint lights only while the half-vector happens to meet that facet -
1296:       // it comes and goes as the camera moves. Firing them all off the surface
1297:       // normal, as the earlier cut did, just puts static white dots on the
1298:       // ground.
1299:       vec2 ovCell${i} = floor(vWorldXZ * ${f(GLITTER_SCALE)});
1300:       vec3 ovFacet${i} = normalize(vec3(
1301:         overlayHash(ovCell${i} + 3.1) - 0.5,
1302:         0.55,
1303:         overlayHash(ovCell${i} + 7.7) - 0.5
1304:       ));
1305:       float ovGlint${i} =
1306:         step(${f(GLITTER_RARITY)}, overlayHash(ovCell${i})) *
1307:         pow(max(dot(ovFacet${i}, ovHalf${i}), 0.0), 90.0);
1308: 
1309:       // Both gated on ovSunOn - an unbound sun direction must not be allowed
1310:       // to put a highlight in an arbitrary place.
1311:       ovCol${i} += vec3(ovWard${i} + ovGlint${i} * ${f(GLITTER_GAIN)}) *
1312:                    ovShade.z * ovSunOn * ovDetail${i};
1313: 
1314:       // Thin snow settles INTO the ground's texture instead of hiding it,
1315:       // which is what lets paving read through at the fringe rather than the
1316:       // layer stopping at a clean edge. Last, so everything above sees the
1317:       // unmodulated amount.
1318:       //
1319:       // The blend runs on a SMOOTHSTEP of depth, not on depth itself, and the
1320:       // difference matters more than it looks. Against raw depth the term
1321:       // never quite reaches 1: at full coverage depth still sits around 0.65
1322:       // in places, which left up to a sixth of the ground texture showing
1323:       // through a snowfield that should have been opaque. The ground texture
1324:       // is a tile magnified across several world units, so what came through
1325:       // was not a hint of stone - it was visible texels. Reaching 1 by a
1326:       // depth of 0.45 confines this to genuinely thin snow, which is the only
1327:       // place it was ever meant to fire.
1328:       ovAmount${i} = clamp(
1329:         ovAmount${i} * mix(
1330:           0.3 + 1.2 * ovHc${i}, 1.0, smoothstep(0.0, 0.45, ovDepth${i})),
1331:         0.0, 1.0);
1332: `;
1333: 
1334:     // The fire skills' melt patches (weather/snowMelt.ts). Branchless: an
1335:     // empty slot carries strength 0, so it contributes nothing to the `max`
1336:     // and the loop costs the same whatever is burning. `length()` of a vec2
1337:     // per slot on a six-slot array is well inside what the ground can spend.
1338:     const meltGlsl = !o.melt
1339:       ? ''
1340:       : `
1341:       float ovMelt${i} = 0.0;
1342:       for (int m = 0; m < ${MELT_SPOTS}; m++) {
1343:         float ovMr${i} = max(ovMeltSpot[m].z, 0.001);
1344:         vec2 ovMv${i} = vWorldXZ - ovMeltSpot[m].xy;
1345: 
1346:         // The patch's own outline rather than a circle. A disc is the one
1347:         // shape that reads as stamped instead of melted, and a fire skill
1348:         // worked over a snowfield left a row of identical coins.
1349:         //
1350:         // The phases are hashed out of the spot's own position, so a patch
1351:         // holds its shape for as long as it lives and no two are alike -
1352:         // without a byte more uniform, because the bearing is the only thing
1353:         // this needs and the loop was already taking the distance.
1354:         float ovMs${i} = fract(
1355:           sin(dot(ovMeltSpot[m].xy, vec2(12.9898, 78.233))) * 43758.5453
1356:         ) * 6.2831853;
1357:         float ovMa${i} = atan(ovMv${i}.y, ovMv${i}.x);
1358:         float ovMk${i} = 1.0
1359:           + ${f(MELT_LOBE_2)} * sin(ovMa${i} * 2.0 + ovMs${i})
1360:           + ${f(MELT_LOBE_3)} * sin(ovMa${i} * 3.0 + ovMs${i} * 1.7)
1361:           + ${f(MELT_LOBE_5)} * sin(ovMa${i} * 5.0 + ovMs${i} * 2.3);
1362: 
1363:         float ovMd${i} = length(ovMv${i}) / (ovMr${i} * ovMk${i});
1364:         ovMelt${i} = max(
1365:           ovMelt${i},
1366:           ovMeltSpot[m].w * (1.0 - smoothstep(${f(MELT_EDGE)}, 1.0, ovMd${i})));
1367:       }
1368: `;
1369: 
1370:     // The uncovered ground, damped as wet. AFTER the layer mix, so it is the
1371:     // GROUND being wetted and not the snow: at the rim of a patch the two
1372:     // overlap and the snow there should still read as snow.
1373:     const meltDampGlsl = !o.melt
1374:       ? ''
1375:       : `
1376:       ${finalColorVar}.rgb *= mix(
1377:         vec3(1.0),
1378:         vec3(${f(MELT_DAMP[0])}, ${f(MELT_DAMP[1])}, ${f(MELT_DAMP[2])}),
1379:         ovMelt${i});`;
1380: 
1381:     return `
1382:     // Layer ${i}: ${o.name} (${o.blend}) - see terrainOverlay.ts.
1383:     if (ovCoverage${i} > 0.0) {
1384:       // Two octaves of value noise decide WHERE the layer lands. Thresholding
1385:       // it against the coverage is what makes the layer creep outward from
1386:       // patches as it builds, instead of the whole ground fading up together.
1387:       vec2 ovP${i} = vWorldXZ * ${f(o.patchScale)};
1388:       float ovBreakup${i} =
1389:           overlayNoise(ovP${i}) * 0.65 + overlayNoise(ovP${i} * 2.7) * 0.35;
1390: ${meltGlsl}
1391:       // Melt divides the TARGET, not the finished amount: everything the
1392:       // layer has - its coverage, its depth, the drifts' relief and lighting,
1393:       // any trail ploughed through it - hangs off this one number, so taking
1394:       // it down here is the difference between snow that was burned away and
1395:       // snow that has been painted over.
1396:       float ovTarget${i} =
1397:           ovCoverage${i} * ${f(1 + (o.headroom ?? 0))} * ${
1398:       o.outdoorOnly ? openVar : '1.0'
1399:     } * ovFlat(${f(o.slopeKnee)}, ${f(o.slopeTop ?? 1)})${o.bed ? ` * ovBedHere${i}` : ''}${
1400:       o.melt ? ` * (1.0 - ovMelt${i})` : ''
1401:     };
1402: 
1403:       float ovAmount${i} = smoothstep(
1404:         ovBreakup${i} - ${f(o.softness)},
1405:         ovBreakup${i} + ${f(o.softness)},
1406:         ovTarget${i}
1407:       );
1408: 
1409:       vec3 ovCol${i} = ${blended};
1410: ${artGlsl}${grainGlsl}${reliefGlsl}
1411:       ${finalColorVar}.rgb = mix(${finalColorVar}.rgb, ovCol${i}, ovAmount${i});${meltDampGlsl}${
1412:       o.reflect
1413:         ? `
1414:       ovWater = max(ovWater, ovAmount${i});`
1415:         : ''
1416:     }${
1417:       o.lightNeutral
1418:         ? `
1419:       ovNeutral = max(ovNeutral, ovAmount${i} * ${f(o.lightNeutral)});`
1420:         : ''
1421:     }
1422:     }`;
1423:   });
1424: 
1425:   // How much of this fragment's light the layers take over. Declared here,
1426:   // outside the per-layer blocks, because the material's final lighting
1427:   // line reads it (terrainOverlayLitGlsl).
1428:   const neutralDecl = hasLightNeutral(overlays)
1429:     ? '    float ovNeutral = 0.0;\n'
1430:     : '';
1431: 
1432:   // Zero when the sun direction never reached the GPU. Every relief term is
1433:   // multiplied by it, so the feature collapses to the flat wash this file drew
1434:   // before it existed - the same rule the coverage uniforms follow: a uniform
1435:   // that fails must fail to "feature off", never to something worse than not
1436:   // having the feature at all.
1437:   const prelude =
1438:     (hasRelief(overlays) || hasReflect(overlays)
1439:       ? '    float ovSunOn = step(0.5, dot(ovSunDir, ovSunDir));\n'
1440:       : '') +
1441:     // How much standing water is under this fragment, read after lighting
1442:     // by terrainOverlayReflectGlsl. Declared outside the layer blocks.
1443:     (hasReflect(overlays) ? '    float ovWater = 0.0;\n' : '');
1444: 
1445:   // Bed share under this fragment, for each layer that has a table. Reads
1446:   // main()'s own locals - the tile indices and the layer-2 alpha the ground
1447:   // was just drawn with - so it lives here rather than in a function.
1448:   const bedHere = overlays
1449:     .map((o, i) =>
1450:       o.bed
1451:         ? `    float ovBedHere${i} = mix(ovBed${i}(int(m1)), ovBed${i}(int(m2)), alphaRendered ? vAlphaColor.a : 0.0);\n`
1452:         : ''
1453:     )
1454:     .join('');
1455: 
1456:   return neutralDecl + bedHere + prelude + blocks.join('\n');
1457: }
1458: 
1459: /** Whether any layer replaces the map's baked light. Allocation-free. */
1460: function hasLightNeutral(overlays: readonly TerrainOverlay[]): boolean {
1461:   for (let i = 0; i < overlays.length; i++) {
1462:     if (overlays[i].lightNeutral) return true;
1463:   }
1464:   return false;
1465: }
1466: 
1467: /**
1468:  * The lighting term the terrain material multiplies its colour by, with the
1469:  * layers' share of it taken over. Emits `vec3 <outVar> = <mapLitExpr>;` and,
1470:  * only when a layer asks for it, a mix toward the layer's own light:
1471:  *
1472:  *   white where the bake is bright and the sun reaches, OVERLAY_LIGHT.shadow
1473:  *   where either is cut. The bake contributes its luminance only - its hue
1474:  *   is the map's own, and the whole point is that snow does not take it.
1475:  *
1476:  * `bakeVar` is the lightmap colour (0…1), `sunVar` the cascaded sun factor
1477:  * (1 = lit), `extraExpr` the light that applies to ground and layer alike
1478:  * (torches, room key) in the same space as `mapLitExpr`. Reads the
1479:  * material's `linearLight` uniform the way the overlay body reads main()'s
1480:  * locals: on the linear tiers the constants are decoded once, the delta is
1481:  * added in linear and the cap applied there (a re-encoded delta is what
1482:  * turned a lamp's footprint into a white diamond on the snow), and the sum is
1483:  * encoded once for the material's final decode, which lands the product
1484:  * exactly at lin(texel) x the linear sum. Must run after `terrainOverlayGlsl`.
1485:  */
1486: export function terrainOverlayLitGlsl(
1487:   overlays: readonly TerrainOverlay[],
1488:   outVar: string,
1489:   mapLitExpr: string,
1490:   bakeVar: string,
1491:   sunVar: string,
1492:   extraExpr: string
1493: ): string {
1494:   if (!hasLightNeutral(overlays)) return `    vec3 ${outVar} = ${mapLitExpr};`;
1495:   const s = OVERLAY_LIGHT.shadow;
1496:   const l = OVERLAY_LIGHT.lit;
1497:   const lin = (c: readonly [number, number, number]) =>
1498:     `vec3(${f(c[0] ** 2.2)}, ${f(c[1] ** 2.2)}, ${f(c[2] ** 2.2)})`;
1499:   return `
1500:     vec3 ${outVar} = ${mapLitExpr};
1501:     {
1502:       float ovBakeLum = dot(${bakeVar}.rgb, vec3(0.299, 0.587, 0.114));
1503:       float ovKey = smoothstep(0.0, 1.0, ovBakeLum * ${f(OVERLAY_LIGHT.gain)}) * ${sunVar};
1504:       vec3 ovExtra = max(${extraExpr}, vec3(0.0));
1505:       vec3 ovLayerLit =
1506:         min(
1507:           mix(vec3(${f(s[0])}, ${f(s[1])}, ${f(s[2])}),
1508:               vec3(${f(l[0])}, ${f(l[1])}, ${f(l[2])}), ovKey) + ovExtra,
1509:           vec3(${f(OVERLAY_LIGHT.cap)}));
1510:       vec3 ovLayerLin =
1511:         min(
1512:           mix(${lin(s)}, ${lin(l)}, ovKey) + pow(ovExtra, vec3(2.2)),
1513:           vec3(${f(OVERLAY_LIGHT.cap ** 2.2)}));
1514:       ovLayerLit = mix(ovLayerLit, pow(ovLayerLin, vec3(1.0 / 2.2)), linearLight);
1515:       ${outVar} = mix(${outVar}, ovLayerLit, ovNeutral);
1516:     }`;
1517: }
1518: 
1519: /**
1520:  * Declarations the overlay body needs. Kept apart from the body so they can sit
1521:  * at file scope in the shader, where GLSL wants functions.
1522:  */
1523: export function terrainOverlayDeclarationsGlsl(
1524:   overlays: readonly TerrainOverlay[]
1525: ): string {
1526:   if (overlays.length === 0) return '';
1527: 
1528:   const uniforms = [
1529:     ...overlays.map((_, i) => `  uniform float ovCoverage${i};`),
1530:     // vec4(worldX, worldZ, radius, strength) per patch; strength 0 = no patch.
1531:     ...(hasMelt(overlays)
1532:       ? [`  uniform vec4 ovMeltSpot[${MELT_SPOTS}];`]
1533:       : []),
1534:   ].join('\n');
1535: 
1536:   // Three more, and only when a layer has a surface. `cameraPosition` is
1537:   // Babylon's own name - ShaderMaterial fills it in for us once it is on the
1538:   // uniform list, which is why terrainOverlayUniforms asks for it there.
1539:   const reliefUniforms = [
1540:     ...(hasRelief(overlays) || hasReflect(overlays)
1541:       ? ['  uniform vec3 ovSunDir;', '  uniform vec3 cameraPosition;']
1542:       : []),
1543:     ...(hasRelief(overlays)
1544:       ? [
1545:           '  uniform vec4 ovShade;',
1546:           '  uniform float ovGrain;',
1547:           ...(hasTrail(overlays)
1548:             ? ['  uniform sampler2D ovTrail;', '  uniform float ovTrailOn;']
1549:             : []),
1550:         ]
1551:       : []),
1552:     ...(hasReflect(overlays)
1553:       ? [
1554:           '  uniform vec3 ovSky;',
1555:           '  uniform vec3 ovSkyHi;',
1556:           '  uniform float ovRipple;',
1557:           '  uniform float ovRain;',
1558:           `  uniform vec4 ovLightPos[${REFLECT_LIGHTS}];`,
1559:           `  uniform vec3 ovLightCol[${REFLECT_LIGHTS}];`,
1560:         ]
1561:       : []),
1562:   ].join('\n');
1563: 
1564:   // Gradient noise, and the reason it exists next to overlayNoise rather than
1565:   // replacing it. (Octave weights are set in the relief function below.)
1566:   //
1567:   // Value noise interpolates a random number per lattice point, so its
1568:   // features line up with the lattice and it reads as soft squares - fine for
1569:   // deciding WHERE a patch of snow lands, where nobody looks at the shape of
1570:   // the boundary, and badly wrong for a surface whose gradient is about to be
1571:   // turned into a normal and lit. On screen the first version of this came out
1572:   // visibly quilted. Gradient noise interpolates a random *direction* instead
1573:   // and has no such alignment.
1574:   //
1575:   // Kept as a second function rather than an upgrade to overlayNoise so the
1576:   // wet-weather layers keep the exact shader they had.
1577:   const gradientNoise = hasRelief(overlays) || hasReflect(overlays)
1578:     ? `
1579:   vec2 ovGrad(vec2 i) {
1580:     // Two hashes into a direction. No trig: normalize of a square-distributed
1581:     // pair is cheaper than a sin/cos pair and the slight bias toward the
1582:     // diagonals disappears once the octaves are summed. The epsilon keeps
1583:     // normalize away from a zero vector on the one lattice point where both
1584:     // hashes land on 0.5.
1585:     vec2 g = vec2(overlayHash(i), overlayHash(i + 17.17)) * 2.0 - 1.0;
1586:     return g / max(length(g), 0.0001);
1587:   }
1588: 
1589:   float ovGnoise(vec2 p) {
1590:     vec2 i = floor(p);
1591:     vec2 f = p - i;
1592:     vec2 u = f * f * (3.0 - 2.0 * f);
1593:     float a = dot(ovGrad(i), f);
1594:     float b = dot(ovGrad(i + vec2(1.0, 0.0)), f - vec2(1.0, 0.0));
1595:     float c = dot(ovGrad(i + vec2(0.0, 1.0)), f - vec2(0.0, 1.0));
1596:     float d = dot(ovGrad(i + vec2(1.0, 1.0)), f - vec2(1.0, 1.0));
1597:     return mix(mix(a, b, u.x), mix(c, d, u.x), u.y) * 0.7 + 0.5;
1598:   }`
1599:     : '';
1600: 
1601:   // Raindrop rings on the standing water. See the RING_* block for what the
1602:   // numbers are; this is the shape.
1603:   //
1604:   // Returned as a SLOPE, not a height, and computed analytically: for a
1605:   // radial profile h(d) the gradient is h'(d) * normalize(p - centre), so the
1606:   // ring never has to be sampled three times to be differenced. That is the
1607:   // whole reason drop rings can sit in the same fragment as the wind ripple
1608:   // without doubling its cost.
1609:   const ringField = hasReflect(overlays)
1610:     ? `
1611:   vec2 ovRingGrad(vec2 p, float t, float salt, float density) {
1612:     vec2 cell = floor(p);
1613: 
1614:     // Each cell keeps its own phase, so the field does not pulse in unison.
1615:     float k = t / ${f(RING_PERIOD)} + overlayHash(cell + salt);
1616:     float period = floor(k);
1617:     float u = k - period;
1618: 
1619:     // Re-rolled every period: whether this cell carries a drop at all, and
1620:     // where in the cell it lands. Without the re-roll the same cells would
1621:     // ring forever and the density could not follow the rain.
1622:     vec2 id = cell + vec2(period * 37.0 + salt, period * 17.0 - salt);
1623:     if (overlayHash(id) > density) return vec2(0.0);
1624: 
1625:     vec2 c = cell + vec2(0.3) +
1626:       0.4 * vec2(overlayHash(id + 5.5), overlayHash(id + 9.1));
1627:     vec2 d = p - c;
1628:     float r = length(d);
1629:     if (r < 0.0001) return vec2(0.0);
1630: 
1631:     // Signed distance from the crest, in crest widths. Compact support: one
1632:     // trough and one crest, nothing outside |x| < 1, which is what keeps the
1633:     // ring inside its own cell.
1634:     float front = u * ${f(RING_MAX)};
1635:     float x = (r - front) / ${f(RING_WIDTH)};
1636:     if (abs(x) >= 1.0) return vec2(0.0);
1637: 
1638:     // h(x) = sin(pi x) (1 - x^2); the derivative is the product rule of it.
1639:     float w = 1.0 - x * x;
1640:     float dh = (3.14159265 * cos(3.14159265 * x) * w -
1641:       2.0 * x * sin(3.14159265 * x)) / ${f(RING_WIDTH)};
1642: 
1643:     // Flattens as it spreads, and is gone before the cell edge.
1644:     float amp = (1.0 - u) * (1.0 - u) *
1645:       (1.0 - smoothstep(${f(RING_FADE)}, ${f(RING_MAX)}, r));
1646: 
1647:     return (d / r) * dh * amp;
1648:   }`
1649:     : '';
1650: 
1651:   // The height of a relief layer's own surface, 0..1.
1652:   //
1653:   // Three octaves, each ROTATED off the last. Rotation is the other half of
1654:   // the fix: octaves stacked on the same axes put their lattices on top of
1655:   // each other and the alignment survives however many you add. 0.8/0.6 is a
1656:   // 37-degree turn, which shares no axis with the ones either side of it.
1657:   const reliefFns = overlays
1658:     .map((o, i) => {
1659:       if (!o.relief) return '';
1660:       const scale = o.reliefScale ?? 1;
1661:       return `
1662:   // Layer ${i} crystal grain: ONE octave, far finer than the drift field, and
1663:   // deliberately kept out of ovRelief so the drift's gradient step does not
1664:   // have to resolve it. Sampling a 0.11-tile period with the drift's 0.2 step
1665:   // would measure nothing but its own aliasing.
1666:   //
1667:   // ANISOTROPIC, and that is the point of it. Undisturbed powder is not
1668:   // covered in round specks - the wind works it into ripples, long in the
1669:   // direction it blew and tight across, and that streaking is most of what
1670:   // separates a snowfield from a noisy white surface. Rotating into the wind
1671:   // axis and then sampling at two very different frequencies gets it for the
1672:   // cost of a 2x2 multiply: low frequency along the wind stretches a feature
1673:   // out, high frequency across it keeps the ripple tight.
1674:   float ovGrainAt${i}(vec2 p) {
1675:     vec2 w = vec2(
1676:       p.x * ${f(WIND_COS)} - p.y * ${f(WIND_SIN)},
1677:       p.x * ${f(WIND_SIN)} + p.y * ${f(WIND_COS)}
1678:     );
1679:     return ovGnoise(vec2(
1680:       w.x * ${f(GRAIN_SCALE * GRAIN_STRETCH)},
1681:       w.y * ${f(GRAIN_SCALE)}
1682:     ));
1683:   }
1684: 
1685:   float ovRelief${i}(vec2 p) {
1686:     vec2 q = p * ${f(scale)};
1687:     float n = ovGnoise(q) * 0.66;
1688:     q = vec2(q.x * 0.8 - q.y * 0.6, q.x * 0.6 + q.y * 0.8) * 2.13;
1689:     n += ovGnoise(q) * 0.23;
1690:     q = vec2(q.x * 0.8 - q.y * 0.6, q.x * 0.6 + q.y * 0.8) * 2.31;
1691:     return n + ovGnoise(q) * 0.11;
1692:   }${
1693:         o.trail
1694:           ? `
1695:   // Depth of the ploughed trail under p, 0..1. Same addressing as the
1696:   // dynamic light map: texel centres on integer tiles.
1697:   float ovTrailAt${i}(vec2 p) {
1698:     return texture2D(ovTrail, (p + 0.5) / 256.0).r * ovTrailOn;
1699:   }
1700: 
1701:   // The trail as a height, in the relief's units: the channel cut down,
1702:   // the rim heaped up along its soft edge, the floor churned.
1703:   float ovTrailShape${i}(vec2 p) {
1704:     float d = ovTrailAt${i}(p);
1705:     // The wall: most of the drop happens over the middle of the ramp, so
1706:     // the channel has a flat floor, a near-vertical side and a flat top,
1707:     // which is what the reference's cut-out edge is.
1708:     float wall = smoothstep(0.12, 0.6, d);
1709:     // The heap of shouldered snow, on the outer shoulder where the depth is
1710:     // still small: a bank raised just outside the cut, not a bump on it.
1711:     float rim = smoothstep(0.0, 0.18, d) * (1.0 - smoothstep(0.18, 0.45, d));
1712:     float churn = (ovGnoise(p * 6.5) - 0.5) * 2.0 * smoothstep(0.5, 0.95, d);
1713:     return -wall * ${f(TRAIL_DEPTH)} + rim * ${f(TRAIL_LIP)} +
1714:            churn * ${f(TRAIL_CHURN)};
1715:   }`
1716:           : ''
1717:       }`;
1718:     })
1719:     .join('');
1720: 
1721:   // The bed table, as a lookup by tile index. An if-chain rather than a
1722:   // const array: GLSL ES 1.00 cannot index a const array by a runtime
1723:   // value, and the terrain shader still has to compile there.
1724:   //
1725:   // The two mapping layers are mixed by the same per-vertex alpha the tile
1726:   // textures are, so the layer thins across a painted path exactly where
1727:   // the path texture fades in. `m1`, `m2`, `alphaRendered` and `vAlphaColor`
1728:   // are the terrain shader's own; this is inlined into its main().
1729:   const bedFns = overlays
1730:     .map((o, i) => {
1731:       if (!o.bed) return '';
1732:       const chain = Object.entries(o.bed)
1733:         .map(([t, s]) => `    if (t == ${t}) return ${f(s)};`)
1734:         .join('\n');
1735:       return `
1736:   float ovBed${i}(int t) {
1737: ${chain}
1738:     return ${f(o.bedDefault ?? 1)};
1739:   }`;
1740:     })
1741:     .join('');
1742: 
1743:   return `
1744: ${uniforms}
1745: ${reliefUniforms}
1746: ${bedFns}
1747: 
1748:   // Every caller of this hashes an INTEGER: the noise lattice hashes
1749:   // floor(p), and the glitter hashes floor(worldXZ * scale). The hash that
1750:   // used to be here could not survive that.
1751:   //
1752:   //     p = fract(p * vec2(127.1, 311.7));
1753:   //
1754:   // For integer x, fract(127.1 * x) is fract(0.1 * x) - ten values, and the
1755:   // same ten for y. So the final mix only ever saw a 10x10 grid of inputs.
1756:   // Measured over a 300x300 patch of lattice: 55 DISTINCT OUTPUTS out of
1757:   // 90000 points, and not one of them above 0.978.
1758:   //
1759:   // Two consequences, both of which had been costing us for a while. Every
1760:   // noise field here - patch placement, drifts, grain - was drawing its values
1761:   // from a set of 55, which is a repeating pattern wearing a noise function's
1762:   // clothes. And any threshold up in the tail, which is how sparse effects
1763:   // like glitter are selected, could never fire at all: it was dead code from
1764:   // the day it was written.
1765:   //
1766:   // This is Hoskins' hash21. On the same lattice: 86136 distinct outputs of
1767:   // 90000, and 2.15% above 0.978 against the 2.2% a uniform hash owes.
1768:   float overlayHash(vec2 p) {
1769:     vec3 p3 = fract(vec3(p.x, p.y, p.x) * 0.1031);
1770:     p3 += dot(p3, p3.yzx + 33.33);
1771:     return fract((p3.x + p3.y) * p3.z);
1772:   }
1773: 
1774:   float overlayNoise(vec2 p) {
1775:     vec2 i = floor(p);
1776:     vec2 f = fract(p);
1777:     f = f * f * (3.0 - 2.0 * f);
1778:     return mix(
1779:       mix(overlayHash(i), overlayHash(i + vec2(1.0, 0.0)), f.x),
1780:       mix(overlayHash(i + vec2(0.0, 1.0)), overlayHash(i + vec2(1.0, 1.0)), f.x),
1781:       f.y
1782:     );
1783:   }
1784: 
1785:   // How much this albedo already IS a layer's own substance: bright, and
1786:   // without the colour that separates blue ice or standing water from snow.
1787:   // The CPU twin is artMatch in terrainOverlay.ts; keep them in step.
1788:   float ovArtMatch(vec3 c, vec2 lumRamp, vec2 chromaRamp) {
1789:     float mx = max(c.r, max(c.g, c.b));
1790:     float mn = min(c.r, min(c.g, c.b));
1791:     float chroma = mx > 0.0 ? (mx - mn) / mx : 0.0;
1792:     float lum = dot(c, vec3(0.2126, 0.7152, 0.0722));
1793:     return smoothstep(lumRamp.x, lumRamp.y, lum) *
1794:            (1.0 - smoothstep(chromaRamp.x, chromaRamp.y, chroma));
1795:   }
1796: 
1797:   // How level this fragment is, against a layer's slope limit.
1798:   // Full by top, not by 1.0: the ground normal is flat per tile, so a
1799:   // ramp that only saturates on dead-level ground leaves every mildly
1800:   // sloped tile half-covered - hard tile-shaped holes across a hillside.
1801:   float ovFlat(float knee, float top) {
1802:     return smoothstep(knee, top, clamp(vNormal.y, 0.0, 1.0));
1803:   }
1804: ${gradientNoise}
1805: ${ringField}
1806: ${reliefFns}
1807:   `;
1808: }
1809: 
1810: /** Per-frame uniform upload. The coverage drivers are read here and nowhere else. */
1811: export function bindTerrainOverlays(
1812:   effect: Effect,
1813:   overlays: readonly TerrainOverlay[],
1814:   scene: Scene
1815: ): void {
1816:   // Zero coverage is the shader's "no layer" path, so an overlay compiled in
1817:   // before the option was switched off simply stops drawing.
1818:   const on = GameOptions.advancedEffects;
1819: 
1820:   for (let i = 0; i < overlays.length; i++) {
1821:     const k = on ? overlays[i].coverage() : 0;
1822:     effect.setFloat(
1823:       `ovCoverage${i}`,
1824:       // NaN out of a driver would poison the mix and paint the ground black;
1825:       // the comparison is false for NaN, so this floors it at zero.
1826:       k > 0 ? Math.min(1, k) : 0
1827:     );
1828:   }
1829: 
1830:   // Before the relief early-out: melting is a coverage term, and a layer that
1831:   // declared it without a surface would still need its patches.
1832:   if (hasMelt(overlays)) {
1833:     effect.setArray4('ovMeltSpot', snowMeltUniform(on));
1834:   }
1835: 
1836:   if (hasReflect(overlays)) bindReflect(effect, overlays, scene, on);
1837: 
1838:   if (!hasRelief(overlays)) return;
1839: 
1840:   const sun = sunDirection(scene);
1841:   effect.setFloat3('ovSunDir', sun.x, sun.y, sun.z);
1842: 
1843:   if (hasTrail(overlays)) {
1844:     effect.setTexture('ovTrail', snowTrailTexture(scene));
1845:     effect.setFloat('ovTrailOn', on && snowTrailPainted() ? 1 : 0);
1846:   }
1847: 
1848:   // Every one of these is a term that contributes nothing at zero, so the
1849:   // advanced-effects switch turns the surface off by handing over zeroes and
1850:   // the layer falls back to the flat wash - no recompile, no reload.
1851:   effect.setFloat('ovGrain', on ? SNOW_SHADE.grain : 0);
1852:   effect.setFloat4(
1853:     'ovShade',
1854:     on ? SNOW_SHADE.relief : 0,
1855:     on ? SNOW_SHADE.cavity : 0,
1856:     on ? SNOW_SHADE.sheen : 0,
1857:     on ? SNOW_SHADE.depth : 0
1858:   );
1859: }
1860: 
1861: /**
1862:  * Standing water's reflections, emitted AFTER the lighting so the water can
1863:  * add light the ground under it never had: a Fresnel share of a built sky,
1864:  * the sun's halo and glint, and each nearby torch as a pin-point glint over
1865:  * a soft glow. Reads `ovWater` (set in the layer block) and `vNormal`,
1866:  * `vWorldPos`; the caller names the lit colour and its sun-shadow factor.
1867:  *
1868:  * Two things happen here, and they are the same thing seen twice.
1869:  *
1870:  * **The normal is water, not ground.** Three fields stack into it: two
1871:  * scrolling octaves of gradient noise for the wind ripple, tilted by
1872:  * `ovRipple`, and - new - the rings of individual raindrops landing on the
1873:  * pool (`ovRingGrad`). Still water is a mirror; in a shower the rings
1874:  * shiver everything it is showing, which is what makes it read as rain ON
1875:  * water instead of a wet decal beside falling rain.
1876:  *
1877:  * **What it reflects is a sky, not a colour.** The mirrored view ray picks a
1878:  * point on a horizon-to-zenith gradient and on a drifting cloud deck above
1879:  * it (see the SKY_/CLOUD_ block). That gradient runs across every pool and
1880:  * moves with the camera, and it is the whole difference between water and a
1881:  * dark patch of paint - the flat `ovSky` wash this had before was, correctly,
1882:  * read as "a texture given to them".
1883:  */
1884: export function terrainOverlayReflectGlsl(
1885:   overlays: readonly TerrainOverlay[],
1886:   colorVar: string,
1887:   sunShadowVar: string
1888: ): string {
1889:   if (!hasReflect(overlays)) return '';
1890: 
1891:   // One recipe's numbers: the first reflecting layer. Maps carry one.
1892:   const o = overlays.find(l => l.reflect)!;
1893:   const r = o.reflect!;
1894: 
1895:   return `
1896:     // ---- standing water (terrainOverlayReflectGlsl) ---------------------
1897:     if (ovWater > 0.0 && ovSunOn > 0.0) {
1898:       vec3 ovV = normalize(cameraPosition - vWorldPos);
1899: 
1900:       vec2 ovRp = vWorldXZ * ${f(RIPPLE_SCALE)};
1901:       vec2 ovR1 = ovRp + vec2(time * 0.7, time * 0.45);
1902:       vec2 ovR2 = ovRp * 2.3 + vec2(-time * 1.1, time * 0.8);
1903:       float ovRh = ovGnoise(ovR1) + 0.5 * ovGnoise(ovR2);
1904:       float ovRhx = ovGnoise(ovR1 + vec2(${f(RIPPLE_STEP * RIPPLE_SCALE)}, 0.0)) +
1905:         0.5 * ovGnoise(ovR2 + vec2(${f(RIPPLE_STEP * RIPPLE_SCALE * 2.3)}, 0.0));
1906:       float ovRhz = ovGnoise(ovR1 + vec2(0.0, ${f(RIPPLE_STEP * RIPPLE_SCALE)})) +
1907:         0.5 * ovGnoise(ovR2 + vec2(0.0, ${f(RIPPLE_STEP * RIPPLE_SCALE * 2.3)}));
1908:       // The gradient is already Δh over one step of the field; dividing by
1909:       // the raw step here scaled it ~17x and tipped every normal nearly
1910:       // horizontal - the whole pool lit up as mottled foil (the "flood"
1911:       // screenshot). 3.0 puts the slope at ~0.3 with full rain on a
1912:       // ripple-0.35 recipe: shivering glints, readable water.
1913:       float ovRk = ovRipple * 3.0;
1914:       vec2 ovSlope = vec2(-(ovRhx - ovRh) * ovRk, -(ovRhz - ovRh) * ovRk);
1915: 
1916:       // The rain landing on the pool. Two ring layers at cell sizes that
1917:       // share no multiple, so the lattice of neither is visible.
1918:       if (ovRain > 0.0) {
1919:         float ovDensity = min(1.0, ovRain * ${f(RING_DENSITY_GAIN)});
1920:         vec2 ovRingP = vWorldXZ * ${f(RING_SCALE)};
1921:         vec2 ovRings =
1922:           ovRingGrad(ovRingP, time, 0.0, ovDensity) +
1923:           0.7 * ovRingGrad(
1924:             ovRingP * ${f(RING_SCALE_2)} + 37.0, time * 1.17, 91.0, ovDensity);
1925:         ovSlope -= ovRings * (ovRain * ${f(RING_SLOPE)});
1926:       }
1927: 
1928:       vec3 ovWn = normalize(vec3(ovSlope.x, 1.0, ovSlope.y));
1929: 
1930:       float ovCosV = max(dot(ovWn, ovV), 0.0);
1931:       float ovF = ${f(REFLECT_F0)} + (1.0 - ${f(REFLECT_F0)}) * pow(1.0 - ovCosV, 5.0);
1932: 
1933:       // ---- the sky in the water ----
1934:       //
1935:       // The mirrored view ray. Steep at the far edge of a pool, grazing at
1936:       // the near one, and shivering wherever a ring crosses it - so every
1937:       // term below varies across the water instead of washing it.
1938:       vec3 ovRay = reflect(-ovV, ovWn);
1939:       float ovUp = clamp(ovRay.y, 0.0, 1.0);
1940:       vec3 ovSkyCol = mix(ovSky, ovSkyHi, pow(ovUp, ${f(SKY_GRADIENT_POW)}));
1941: 
1942:       // Where that ray meets the cloud deck. Dividing by the ray's elevation
1943:       // is the parallax: a grazing ray travels much further before it gets
1944:       // there, so the deck stretches toward the near edge of the pool and
1945:       // swims when the camera moves.
1946:       vec2 ovDeck = vWorldXZ + ovRay.xz * (${f(CLOUD_HEIGHT)} / max(ovRay.y, 0.08));
1947:       vec2 ovCloudP =
1948:         (ovDeck + vec2(time * ${f(CLOUD_DRIFT)}, time * ${f(CLOUD_DRIFT * 0.6)})) *
1949:         ${f(CLOUD_SCALE)};
1950:       float ovCloud =
1951:         ovGnoise(ovCloudP) * 0.62 + ovGnoise(ovCloudP * 2.7 + 13.0) * 0.38;
1952:       // Only where the water is actually showing sky: a near-horizontal ray
1953:       // is looking at the far ground, and putting clouds there is what a
1954:       // scrolling texture would do.
1955:       ovCloud = smoothstep(${f(CLOUD_LO)}, ${f(CLOUD_HI)}, ovCloud) * ovUp;
1956:       ovSkyCol *= mix(1.0, ${f(CLOUD_LIT)}, ovCloud);
1957: 
1958:       vec3 ovRefl = ovSkyCol * ${f(r.sky)} * ovF;
1959: 
1960:       // The sun, cut by its own shadow so a puddle under a wall stays dark.
1961:       //
1962:       // The halo is the bright patch of SKY around the sun, so it is tested
1963:       // on the mirrored ray - where the water is looking - and not on the
1964:       // half vector. On a near-level pool the half vector barely changes
1965:       // across the whole surface, so a halo built on it is another flat
1966:       // wash; on the ray it swings with the ripple and slides as you walk,
1967:       // which is the point of having it at all.
1968:       ovRefl += ovSkyCol *
1969:         pow(max(dot(ovRay, ovSunDir), 0.0), ${f(SUN_HALO_GLOSS)}) *
1970:         ${f(SUN_HALO)} * ${sunShadowVar} * max(ovF, 0.25);
1971: 
1972:       // The pin-point glint stays on the half vector: that one is a
1973:       // specular highlight on the surface, not a piece of sky.
1974:       vec3 ovH = normalize(ovV + ovSunDir);
1975:       ovRefl += vec3(1.0) * pow(max(dot(ovWn, ovH), 0.0), ${f(r.gloss)}) *
1976:         ${f(SUN_GLINT)} * ${sunShadowVar} * max(ovF, 0.25);
1977: 
1978:       // The torches: a glint and, under it, the smeared glow of the flame.
1979:       for (int i = 0; i < ${REFLECT_LIGHTS}; i++) {
1980:         vec3 ovL = ovLightPos[i].xyz - vWorldPos;
1981:         float ovD = length(ovL);
1982:         ovL /= max(ovD, 0.001);
1983:         float ovAtt = max(0.0, 1.0 - ovD / max(ovLightPos[i].w, 0.001));
1984:         ovAtt *= ovAtt;
1985:         vec3 ovLh = normalize(ovV + ovL);
1986:         float ovNdh = max(dot(ovWn, ovLh), 0.0);
1987:         ovRefl += ovLightCol[i] * ovAtt * (
1988:           pow(ovNdh, ${f(r.gloss)}) * ${f(TORCH_GLINT)} +
1989:           pow(ovNdh, ${f(TORCH_GLOW_GLOSS)}) * ${f(TORCH_GLOW)}
1990:         ) * max(ovF, 0.25);
1991:       }
1992: 
1993:       // Bounded: three torches and the sun on one grazing fragment must
1994:       // bloom, not white the pool out.
1995:       ${colorVar} += min(ovRefl, vec3(1.5)) * ovWater;
1996:     }
1997: `;
1998: }
1999: 
2000: /** The pool's lights and the sky colour, for the water. */
2001: function bindReflect(
2002:   effect: Effect,
2003:   overlays: readonly TerrainOverlay[],
2004:   scene: Scene,
2005:   on: boolean
2006: ): void {
2007:   if (!hasRelief(overlays)) {
2008:     const sun = sunDirection(scene);
2009:     effect.setFloat3('ovSunDir', sun.x, sun.y, sun.z);
2010:   }
2011: 
2012:   // The HORIZON the water reflects: the director's haze colour, which is
2013:   // what the map's own distance fades to; with no haze, the clear colour is
2014:   // the sky. Both arrive linear when the buffer is, and this term is added
2015:   // in the shader's display-space sum ahead of the final decode, so they are
2016:   // re-encoded here. Black either way is "reflects nothing", which is safe.
2017:   const look = lookDirector()?.state();
2018:   const linear = linearBufferActive(scene);
2019:   const encode = (v: number) => (linear ? Math.pow(Math.max(v, 0), 1 / 2.2) : v);
2020:   const fog = look && look.profile.fog.density > 0 ? look.fogColorLinear : null;
2021:   const clear = scene.clearColor;
2022:   const horizon: readonly [number, number, number] = fog
2023:     ? [encode(fog[0]), encode(fog[1]), encode(fog[2])]
2024:     : [encode(clear.r), encode(clear.g), encode(clear.b)];
2025: 
2026:   effect.setFloat3(
2027:     'ovSky',
2028:     on ? horizon[0] : 0,
2029:     on ? horizon[1] : 0,
2030:     on ? horizon[2] : 0
2031:   );
2032: 
2033:   // The ZENITH: the key's sky light, which is the colour everything on the
2034:   // map is already being lit from above by, so the water agrees with the
2035:   // scene rather than inventing a second sky.
2036:   //
2037:   // Held apart from the horizon by at least ZENITH_SEPARATION, and never
2038:   // above it: a map whose sky light and haze happen to match would otherwise
2039:   // reflect a flat colour again - the exact failure the gradient exists to
2040:   // fix - and the sky is darker overhead than at the horizon.
2041:   const key = look?.key;
2042:   for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
2043:     const zenith = key ? key.skyColor[i] * key.skyIntensity : 0;
2044:     skyHi[i] = on
2045:       ? Math.min(zenith, horizon[i] * (1 - ZENITH_SEPARATION))
2046:       : 0;
2047:   }
2048:   effect.setFloat3('ovSkyHi', skyHi[0], skyHi[1], skyHi[2]);
2049: 
2050:   const o = overlays.find(l => l.reflect)!;
2051:   const rain = rainStrength();
2052:   effect.setFloat('ovRipple', on ? rain * o.reflect!.ripple : 0);
2053:   // The drop rings read the rain directly, not the recipe's ripple tuning:
2054:   // how many drops are landing is a fact about the sky, not about how glossy
2055:   // this particular map's water was dialled.
2056:   effect.setFloat('ovRain', on ? rain : 0);
2057: 
2058:   const lights = pointLightPoolLights();
2059:   for (let i = 0; i < REFLECT_LIGHTS; i++) {
2060:     const l = lights[i];
2061:     const k = on && l && l.intensity > 0 ? l.intensity : 0;
2062:     reflectPos[i * 4] = l ? l.position.x : 0;
2063:     reflectPos[i * 4 + 1] = l ? l.position.y : 0;
2064:     reflectPos[i * 4 + 2] = l ? l.position.z : 0;
2065:     reflectPos[i * 4 + 3] = l ? l.range : 0;
2066:     reflectCol[i * 3] = l ? l.diffuse.r * k : 0;
2067:     reflectCol[i * 3 + 1] = l ? l.diffuse.g * k : 0;
2068:     reflectCol[i * 3 + 2] = l ? l.diffuse.b * k : 0;
2069:   }
2070:   effect.setArray4('ovLightPos', reflectPos);
2071:   effect.setArray3('ovLightCol', reflectCol);
2072: }
2073: 
2074: const reflectPos: number[] = new Array(REFLECT_LIGHTS * 4).fill(0);
2075: const reflectCol: number[] = new Array(REFLECT_LIGHTS * 3).fill(0);
2076: const skyHi: [number, number, number] = [0, 0, 0];
</file>

<file path="src/libs/mu/terrainParse.worker.ts">
  1: /// <reference lib="webworker" />
  2: 
  3: /**
  4:  * Terrain parsing off the main thread (todo C8).
  5:  *
  6:  * Every parser reachable from here is pure: typed arrays in, typed arrays
  7:  * out, no Babylon and no DOM. That is why `mapFileEncryption` and friends
  8:  * import `common/binaryUtils` rather than `common/utils` - the latter pulls
  9:  * in `Scene`/`Texture` and would drag the whole engine into this chunk.
 10:  *
 11:  * Two request kinds rather than one, because the light bake depends on a
 12:  * JPEG decode that only the engine can do:
 13:  *
 14:  *   'bulk'  - height + attributes + mapping + objects, straight from the
 15:  *             downloaded bytes. This is the part that used to block the
 16:  *             frame on every map change.
 17:  *   'light' - the normal/luminosity pass, once the main thread has decoded
 18:  *             TerrainLight.OZJ into floats.
 19:  */
 20: 
 21: import { parseTerrainAttribute } from '../../common/terrain/parseTerrainAttribute';
 22: import { parseTerrainHeight } from '../../common/terrain/parseTerrainHeight';
 23: import { parseTerrainMapping } from '../../common/terrain/parseTerrainMapping';
 24: import { parseTerrainObjects } from '../../common/terrain/parseTerrainObjects';
 25: import { parseTerrainLightPacked } from '../../common/terrain/parseTerrainLight';
 26: import type { ENUM_WORLD } from '../../common/types';
 27: 
 28: export type TerrainWorkerRequest =
 29:   | {
 30:       id: number;
 31:       kind: 'bulk';
 32:       map: ENUM_WORLD;
 33:       heightBytes: Uint8Array;
 34:       attributeBytes: Uint8Array;
 35:       mappingBytes: Uint8Array;
 36:       objectBytes: Uint8Array;
 37:     }
 38:   | {
 39:       id: number;
 40:       kind: 'light';
 41:       lightBuffer: Float32Array;
 42:       heightData: Float32Array;
 43:       liftBorder: boolean;
 44:     };
 45: 
 46: export type TerrainWorkerBulkResult = {
 47:   height: Float32Array;
 48:   attributes: Uint16Array;
 49:   layer1: Uint8Array;
 50:   layer2: Uint8Array;
 51:   alpha: Uint8Array;
 52:   objects: ReturnType<typeof parseTerrainObjects>;
 53: };
 54: 
 55: export type TerrainWorkerResponse =
 56:   | { id: number; ok: true; kind: 'bulk'; result: TerrainWorkerBulkResult }
 57:   | { id: number; ok: true; kind: 'light'; result: Float32Array }
 58:   | { id: number; ok: false; error: string };
 59: 
 60: const ctx = self as unknown as DedicatedWorkerGlobalScope;
 61: 
 62: ctx.onmessage = async (ev: MessageEvent<TerrainWorkerRequest>) => {
 63:   const req = ev.data;
 64: 
 65:   try {
 66:     if (req.kind === 'bulk') {
 67:       const height = await parseTerrainHeight(req.heightBytes);
 68:       const attributes = await parseTerrainAttribute(
 69:         req.attributeBytes,
 70:         req.map
 71:       );
 72:       const mapping = await parseTerrainMapping(req.mappingBytes);
 73:       const objects = parseTerrainObjects(req.objectBytes);
 74: 
 75:       const result: TerrainWorkerBulkResult = {
 76:         height,
 77:         attributes,
 78:         layer1: mapping.layer1,
 79:         layer2: mapping.layer2,
 80:         alpha: mapping.alpha,
 81:         objects,
 82:       };
 83: 
 84:       // Transfer the buffers rather than copying them: ~850 KB per map change.
 85:       ctx.postMessage({ id: req.id, ok: true, kind: 'bulk', result }, [
 86:         height.buffer,
 87:         attributes.buffer,
 88:         mapping.layer1.buffer,
 89:         mapping.layer2.buffer,
 90:         mapping.alpha.buffer,
 91:       ]);
 92:       return;
 93:     }
 94: 
 95:     const packed = parseTerrainLightPacked(
 96:       req.lightBuffer,
 97:       req.heightData,
 98:       req.liftBorder
 99:     );
100: 
101:     ctx.postMessage({ id: req.id, ok: true, kind: 'light', result: packed }, [
102:       packed.buffer,
103:     ]);
104:   } catch (error) {
105:     ctx.postMessage({
106:       id: req.id,
107:       ok: false,
108:       error: error instanceof Error ? error.message : String(error),
109:     });
110:   }
111: };
</file>

<file path="src/libs/mu/terrainParseClient.ts">
  1: /**
  2:  * Main-thread half of the terrain parsing worker (todo C8).
  3:  *
  4:  * One worker, created lazily on the first map load and kept for the session
  5:  * (map changes reuse it). If the worker cannot be constructed - no module
  6:  * worker support, a bundler quirk, a CSP - every call falls back to running
  7:  * the same parsers inline, which is exactly the old behaviour, so this can
  8:  * only ever cost the stutter it was meant to remove.
  9:  */
 10: 
 11: import { parseTerrainAttribute } from '../../common/terrain/parseTerrainAttribute';
 12: import { parseTerrainHeight } from '../../common/terrain/parseTerrainHeight';
 13: import { parseTerrainMapping } from '../../common/terrain/parseTerrainMapping';
 14: import { parseTerrainObjects } from '../../common/terrain/parseTerrainObjects';
 15: import { parseTerrainLightPacked } from '../../common/terrain/parseTerrainLight';
 16: import type { ENUM_WORLD } from '../../common/types';
 17: import type {
 18:   TerrainWorkerBulkResult,
 19:   TerrainWorkerRequest,
 20:   TerrainWorkerResponse,
 21: } from './terrainParse.worker';
 22: 
 23: /**
 24:  * Escape hatch, in the style of `USE_TILE_TEXTURE_ARRAY`: set to false to
 25:  * parse everything on the main thread exactly as before the worker landed.
 26:  * The two paths call the same functions with the same inputs, so this only
 27:  * changes *where* the work runs.
 28:  */
 29: const USE_TERRAIN_WORKER = true;
 30: 
 31: let worker: Worker | null | undefined;
 32: let nextId = 1;
 33: 
 34: const pending = new Map<
 35:   number,
 36:   { resolve: (value: any) => void; reject: (reason: unknown) => void }
 37: >();
 38: 
 39: function getWorker(): Worker | null {
 40:   if (!USE_TERRAIN_WORKER) return null;
 41:   if (worker !== undefined) return worker;
 42: 
 43:   try {
 44:     worker = new Worker(new URL('./terrainParse.worker.ts', import.meta.url), {
 45:       type: 'module',
 46:       name: 'terrain-parse',
 47:     });
 48: 
 49:     worker.onmessage = (ev: MessageEvent<TerrainWorkerResponse>) => {
 50:       const msg = ev.data;
 51:       const entry = pending.get(msg.id);
 52:       if (!entry) return;
 53:       pending.delete(msg.id);
 54: 
 55:       if (msg.ok) entry.resolve(msg.result);
 56:       else entry.reject(new Error(msg.error));
 57:     };
 58: 
 59:     worker.onerror = event => {
 60:       console.warn('Terrain worker failed, parsing inline instead:', event);
 61:       // Fail every outstanding call so the caller's fallback runs; drop the
 62:       // worker so later map loads go straight to the inline path.
 63:       for (const [, entry] of pending) {
 64:         entry.reject(new Error('terrain worker error'));
 65:       }
 66:       pending.clear();
 67:       worker?.terminate();
 68:       worker = null;
 69:     };
 70:   } catch (error) {
 71:     console.warn('Terrain worker unavailable, parsing inline instead:', error);
 72:     worker = null;
 73:   }
 74: 
 75:   return worker;
 76: }
 77: 
 78: /**
 79:  * Inputs are structured-cloned, never transferred. Transferring them would
 80:  * be ~1.2 MB cheaper per map change, but it detaches the caller's buffers -
 81:  * and the worker can still fail *after* the post (onerror mid-flight), at
 82:  * which point the inline fallback would be handed detached arrays and throw.
 83:  * A one-off clone of ~1.2 MB is not worth trading a working fallback for.
 84:  * Results travel the other way transferred, where no fallback is at stake.
 85:  */
 86: function post<T>(req: TerrainWorkerRequest): Promise<T> {
 87:   const w = getWorker();
 88:   if (!w) return Promise.reject(new Error('no terrain worker'));
 89: 
 90:   return new Promise<T>((resolve, reject) => {
 91:     pending.set(req.id, { resolve, reject });
 92:     w.postMessage(req);
 93:   });
 94: }
 95: 
 96: /** The inline equivalent of a 'bulk' request, used when the worker is out. */
 97: async function bulkInline(
 98:   map: ENUM_WORLD,
 99:   heightBytes: Uint8Array,
100:   attributeBytes: Uint8Array,
101:   mappingBytes: Uint8Array,
102:   objectBytes: Uint8Array
103: ): Promise<TerrainWorkerBulkResult> {
104:   const height = await parseTerrainHeight(heightBytes);
105:   const attributes = await parseTerrainAttribute(attributeBytes, map);
106:   const mapping = await parseTerrainMapping(mappingBytes);
107: 
108:   return {
109:     height,
110:     attributes,
111:     layer1: mapping.layer1,
112:     layer2: mapping.layer2,
113:     alpha: mapping.alpha,
114:     objects: parseTerrainObjects(objectBytes),
115:   };
116: }
117: 
118: /**
119:  * Height, attributes, tile mapping and the object list in one round trip.
120:  *
121:  * The four byte arrays are cloned into the worker, so the caller keeps
122:  * ownership of them.
123:  */
124: export async function parseTerrainBulk(
125:   map: ENUM_WORLD,
126:   heightBytes: Uint8Array,
127:   attributeBytes: Uint8Array,
128:   mappingBytes: Uint8Array,
129:   objectBytes: Uint8Array
130: ): Promise<TerrainWorkerBulkResult> {
131:   try {
132:     return await post<TerrainWorkerBulkResult>({
133:       id: nextId++,
134:       kind: 'bulk',
135:       map,
136:       heightBytes,
137:       attributeBytes,
138:       mappingBytes,
139:       objectBytes,
140:     });
141:   } catch (error) {
142:     console.warn('Terrain bulk parse fell back to the main thread:', error);
143:     return bulkInline(
144:       map,
145:       heightBytes,
146:       attributeBytes,
147:       mappingBytes,
148:       objectBytes
149:     );
150:   }
151: }
152: 
153: /**
154:  * The baked light pass. Both inputs are cloned; the caller keeps using
155:  * `heightData` for the ground mesh and the height map.
156:  */
157: export async function parseTerrainLightOffThread(
158:   lightBuffer: Float32Array,
159:   heightData: Float32Array,
160:   liftBorder: boolean
161: ): Promise<Float32Array> {
162:   try {
163:     return await post<Float32Array>({
164:       id: nextId++,
165:       kind: 'light',
166:       lightBuffer,
167:       heightData,
168:       liftBorder,
169:     });
170:   } catch (error) {
171:     console.warn('Terrain light parse fell back to the main thread:', error);
172:     return parseTerrainLightPacked(lightBuffer, heightData, liftBorder);
173:   }
174: }
