 156:  * The grass took none of it, so blades measured 0.84 of the ground they stood
 157:  * in and read as dark blue streaks over a white field. Snow that lies on the
 158:  * ground lies on what grows out of it too: the blade takes the same coverage,
 159:  * whitening toward the overlay's own colour and shortening as it is buried.
 160:  *
 161:  * Following terrainOverlay's rule, the colour is a literal and only the
 162:  * coverage is a uniform, so a uniform that never reaches the GPU reads as
 163:  * zero and means *no snow* - the safe state, not a white field.
 164:  */
 165: const SNOW_COLOUR = SNOW_COVER.colour;
 166: /** How far toward snow-white a blade goes at full cover. */
 167: const SNOW_ON_GRASS = 0.85;
 168: /** What is left of a blade's height at full cover: the rest is buried. */
 169: const SNOW_BURY = 0.55;
 170: 
 171: /**
 172:  * The detailed blade's resting arc: how far the tip leans out, as a fraction
 173:  * of the blade's height, from nearly upright to well over. The spread is the
 174:  * point - a field where every blade curves the same amount reads as combed.
 175:  */
 176: const ARC_MIN = 0.15;
 177: const ARC_MAX = 0.85;
 178: /** How much of the lean comes out of the blade's height (it cannot do both). */
 179: const ARC_DROOP = 0.45;
 180: 
 181: /**
 182:  * Fire, as the blade sees it (`weather/grassBurn.ts` owns the scar itself).
 183:  *
 184:  * Disintegration rather than a shrink. Each blade goes when the burn at its
 185:  * root passes *its own* threshold, drawn from the hash it already carries, so
 186:  * a patch thins out blade by blade instead of sinking like a sheet - and
 187:  * because the threshold is a pure function of the blade, the same blades
 188:  * always go first and the edge does not crawl about as the map decays.
 189:  *
 190:  * Thresholds run over MIN to MIN + SPREAD on the *cube* of the hash, and the
 191:  * curve is the point. SPREAD carries the top of the range past 1, so a fully
 192:  * burnt patch keeps the blades whose threshold the fire never reached and
 193:  * leaves them standing and charred - with every threshold under 1 a full burn
 194:  * took every blade and the scar was bare ground, which reads as a hole in the
 195:  * field rather than as something burnt. But spreading them evenly to get that
 196:  * stubble left a third of the field standing and the scar stopped reading at
 197:  * all. Cubed, about three quarters go outright and a sixth stay: a clear scar
 198:  * with something burnt still standing in it. `WITHER` is how much
 199:  * more burn it takes for a blade to go from whole to gone, which is what
 200:  * keeps the edge soft at a scale finer than the map's 25 cm texel.
 201:  */
 202: const BURN_MIN = 0.05;
 203: const BURN_SPREAD = 0.8;
 204: const BURN_WITHER = 0.25;
 205: 
 206: /**
 207:  * What a blade chars toward on its way out, and how far it gets. Not quite
 208:  * black: charred grass keeps a little brown, and true black against Lorencia's
 209:  * ground reads as a hole in the terrain rather than as burnt.
 210:  */
 211: const CHAR_COLOUR = [0.045, 0.037, 0.032] as const;
 212: const CHAR_TINT = 0.96;
 213: 
 214: /**
 215:  * The dissolve edge travelling down a burning blade.
 216:  *
 217:  * `RIM` is how much of the blade is glowing ember at the cut and `CHAR` how
 218:  * much is blackened behind it, both as a fraction of the blade's length. The
 219:  * rim is deliberately thin: it is a line of fire, and widening it turns the
 220:  * whole blade orange, which reads as a lit blade rather than a burning one.
 221:  *
 222:  * `WOBBLE` and `FREQ` bend the cut per blade off its own hash, so a burning
 223:  * patch does not go as one straight line drawn across the field.
 224:  */
 225: /**
 226:  * Burn value above which a cut still glows. The scar starts at 1 and decays
 227:  * over the five minutes it takes to grow back, so this is a time in disguise.
 228:  *
 229:  * Tight on purpose: at about two and a half seconds, and a front travelling
 230:  * half a tile a second, the band of blades that are actually alight is a tile
 231:  * or so wide - a flame front crossing the field. Wider and the whole scar
 232:  * burns at once, which is a lit patch rather than a fire going through it.
 233:  *
 234:  * It cannot go much tighter. The scar is one byte decaying over five minutes,
 235:  * so a step of the counter is over a second however often the pass runs, and
 236:  * a window narrower than a couple of steps would flicker on the quantisation
 237:  * rather than on the flame.
 238:  */
 239: const HEAT_FROM = 0.992;
 240: 
 241: /**
 242:  * Fraction of stubs that keep a lit tip while the scar is hot.
 243:  *
 244:  * Every cut glowing turns a burnt patch into a hillside of birthday candles -
 245:  * a field of identical orange points, one per blade, which is nothing like an
 246:  * ember. Real ones are scattered and few. Picked off the blade hash so the
 247:  * same stubs glow for as long as the heat lasts rather than twinkling.
 248:  */
 249: const EMBER_CHANCE = 0.13;
 250: 
 251: const DISSOLVE_RIM = 0.055;
 252: const DISSOLVE_CHAR = 0.22;
 253: const DISSOLVE_WOBBLE = 0.045;
 254: const DISSOLVE_FREQ = 7;
 255: 
 256: /**
 257:  * Burn at which a blade is fully blackened, whatever the dissolve is doing to
 258:  * it.
 259:  *
 260:  * This is separate from the dissolve threshold on purpose, and running them
 261:  * together was the mistake. A blade whose threshold the fire never reached was
 262:  * left standing at *full height and still green*, in the middle of a black
 263:  * patch - so a burnt field read as a field with some grass missing from it
 264:  * rather than as a burnt field. Every blade the fire touches goes black; the
 265:  * threshold only decides how much of its length it loses.
 266:  */
 267: const CHAR_AT = 0.3;
 268: 
 269: /**
 270:  * How much of its length a blade can lose, least to most, spread per blade.
 271:  *
 272:  * Capped below 1: a blade that dissolves away entirely leaves nothing behind,
 273:  * and what a burnt field is made of is black stalks of uneven height. So the
 274:  * fire takes the top of each one and leaves the stub.
 275:  */
 276: const DISSOLVE_MIN = 0.45;
 277: const DISSOLVE_SPAN = 0.45;
 278: 
 279: /**
 280:  * The flame drawn on a burning blade, in place of a sprite.
 281:  *
 282:  * LEN is how far down the leaf the tongue reaches, as a fraction of its
 283:  * length; the two RATEs and FREQ are the flicker, out of phase so no two
 284:  * blades are in step. LOW and HIGH are the body and the hot core - both over
 285:  * 1, so the bloom pass has something to take, which is what makes a small
 286:  * flame read as bright rather than as an orange smear.
 287:  */
 288: const FLAME_LEN = 0.26;
 289: const FLAME_RATE = [7.3, 11.9] as const;
 290: const FLAME_FREQ = 9;
 291: const FLAME_LOW = [2.2, 0.55, 0.08] as const;
 292: const FLAME_HIGH = [3.0, 1.35, 0.3] as const;
 293: 
 294: /** The ember line itself. Above 1 so the bloom pass has something to catch. */
 295: const EMBER_COLOUR = [2.6, 0.85, 0.18] as const;
 296: 
 297: /** Tufts per tile, and how far a blade strays from its tuft, in tiles. */
 298: const CLUMPS_PER_TILE = 4;
 299: const CLUMP_SPREAD = 0.34;
 300: 
 301: /**
 302:  * The Anime outline (renderingStyle.ts): the side lines never take more than
 303:  * this share of the blade's half width, and they fade out below this point
 304:  * along the blade (root 0, tip 1), baked into the shader text.
 305:  */
 306: const GRASS_INK_MAX_SHARE = 0.75;
 307: const GRASS_INK_START_GLSL = grassInkStart().toFixed(2);
 308: 
 309: /**
 310:  * Form shading on the detailed blade: how far a face turned toward the sun
 311:  * sits above one turned away.
 312:  *
 313:  * This is the difference between a field of curved shapes and a field of
 314:  * grass - without it every blade is the same value and the whole thing reads
 315:  * flat. It is written to be **albedo-preserving**: the term runs from
 316:  * `1 - FORM` to `1 + FORM` across `0.5 + 0.5 * dot(n, sun)`, and blade yaw is
 317:  * a uniform hash, so the field's mean is 1 and the layer neither brightens
 318:  * nor darkens the frame.
 319:  *
 320:  * That property is what keeps it out of the lighting model. It redistributes
 321:  * light across a blade's own two faces - which flat ground does not have and
 322:  * a lightmap cannot express - rather than adding a second opinion about how
 323:  * much light is here. `terrainLighting.ts` remains the only writer of that.
 324:  */
 325: const FORM_SHADE = 0.34;
 326: /** How far the fragment rounds the normal across the blade's width. */
 327: const BLADE_ROUND = 0.55;
 328: 
 329: /**
 330:  * How far a blade is pulled toward the colour of the light passing *through*
 331:  * it, at the tip where it is thinnest.
 332:  *
 333:  * A blade is `albedo x groundLit`, and a saturated green albedo has almost no
 334:  * blue in it: a violet spell can light the tile, the dirt path and the pale
 335:  * props around it and the field still reads green, because green is all the
 336:  * albedo can reflect. That is right for reflection and wrong for a blade,
 337:  * which is thin enough to pass light rather than only bounce it, and what
 338:  * comes through carries the emitter's colour and not the leaf's.
 339:  *
 340:  * Kept honest by three things: the pull is toward the emitter hue *at the
 341:  * blade's own luma*, so it recolours and never brightens; it is scaled by the
 342:  * emitters' share of the light on this tile, so daylight alone does nothing
 343:  * and the term is inert on a map with no dynamic light; and it runs to zero
 344:  * at the root, where a blade is thick and sits in its own shadow.
 345:  *
 346:  * `terrainLighting.ts` is still the only writer of how much light is here -
 347:  * this spends `extraLit`, it does not add to it. Dev seam: `?grasstransmit=`.
 348:  */
 349: const GRASS_TRANSMIT = 0.6;
 350: 
 351: const transmitDev = devQueryNumber('grasstransmit');
 352: 
 353: /** 0 on Classic, like `lightTintStrength`: that tier gets no modern term. */
 354: function grassTransmitStrength(): number {
 355:   if (transmitDev !== null) return Math.max(0, transmitDev);
 356: 
 357:   return tierIndex() >= 1 ? GRASS_TRANSMIT : 0;
 358: }
 359: 
 360: /**
 361:  * What walks through the grass and what flies over it.
 362:  *
 363:  * The cheapest thing that does this job: a short uniform array of the nearest
 364:  * actors, tested in the vertex shader. No second texture, no per-frame
 365:  * upload, no CPU pass over the field - the only cost is `GRASS_ACTORS`
 366:  * distance tests per vertex, on the detailed blade alone.
 367:  *
 368:  * The alternative was a trample map in the `terrainDynamicLight` mould: a
 369:  * texture actors stamp and grass samples. It buys a *trail* - grass that
 370:  * stays flat behind you and springs back - and unlimited actors, for one
 371:  * fetch instead of eight tests. It also costs a texture, a per-frame upload
 372:  * and a decay pass, and it needs a moving window to have the resolution a
 373:  * one-tile-wide character needs. Worth revisiting if the springback is
 374:  * wanted; not the cheapest way to get what was asked for.
 375:  */
 376: const GRASS_ACTORS = 16;
 377: 
 378: /**
 379:  * Grass does not spring back the instant a foot leaves it, and a press that
 380:  * vanishes on the frame the player steps off pops.
 381:  *
 382:  * So the slots are not actors, they are *presses*: a body stamps one where it
 383:  * stands, keeps it alive while it is there, and every press fades over
 384:  * `PRESS_LIFE` once nothing is refreshing it. A body that moves further than
 385:  * `STAMP_STEP` from the nearest press stamps another, which leaves a short
 386:  * trail behind a run that recovers from the back.
 387:  *
 388:  * This is the trample map's springback for the price already being paid -
 389:  * the same uniform array, the same loop - because the persistence lives in
 390:  * ten CPU-side records rather than in a texture.
 391:  */
 392: const STAMP_STEP = 0.75;
 393: /** Seconds a press takes to recover once nothing stands on it. */
 394: const PRESS_LIFE = 4.5;
 395: 
 396: /**
 397:  * How many bodies may stamp, out of `GRASS_ACTORS` slots. The rest of the
 398:  * slots are the trail they leave: with the budget equal to the slot count
 399:  * there was no room for one, and a crowd of monsters could take every slot
 400:  * from the player.
 401:  */
 402: const ACTOR_BUDGET = 4;
 403: 
 404: /**
 405:  * Slots left for the trail once every body has its live press.
 406:  *
 407:  * The first `ACTOR_BUDGET` slots are written fresh each frame at wherever the
 408:  * bodies are; the rest hold the breadcrumbs they have left behind, which is
 409:  * the part that fades.
 410:  */
 411: const TRAIL_SLOTS = GRASS_ACTORS - ACTOR_BUDGET;
 412: 
 413: /** The `n` entries closest to a point, without sorting or allocating. */
 414: function nearest(
 415:   actors: readonly GrassActor[],
 416:   cx: number,
 417:   cz: number,
 418:   n: number
 419: ): GrassActor[] {
 420:   picked.length = 0;
 421: 
 422:   for (const a of actors) {
 423:     const d = (a.x - cx) ** 2 + (a.z - cz) ** 2;
 424: 
 425:     let at = picked.length;
 426:     while (at > 0 && picked[at - 1].d > d) at--;
 427: 
 428:     if (at >= n) continue;
 429: 
 430:     picked.splice(at, 0, { a, d });
 431:     if (picked.length > n) picked.length = n;
 432:   }
 433: 
 434:   return picked.map(p => p.a);
 435: }
 436: 
 437: const picked: { a: GrassActor; d: number }[] = [];
 438: /**
 439:  * Tiles from an actor at which the grass is untouched.
 440:  *
 441:  * A tile is a metre and a body is not: 1.6 cleared a 3-metre bubble around
 442:  * the player, which reads as a force field rather than as feet. This is a
 443:  * footprint plus the swing of a leg, and the falloff below starts at 0.6 of
 444:  * it so the edge is a press rather than a wall.
 445:  */
 446: const ACTOR_RADIUS = 0.9;
 447: /** Where the press begins, as a fraction of the radius. */
 448: const ACTOR_CORE = 0.35;
 449: /** How flat a blade goes directly under someone standing on it. */
 450: const TRAMPLE_FLATTEN = 0.8;
 451: /** How far a blade is shoved aside, as a fraction of its height. */
 452: const TRAMPLE_PUSH = 0.8;
 453: /** A flier's downwash reaches further and presses less. */
 454: const FLIER_RADIUS = 2.0;
 455: const FLIER_PUSH = 0.9;
 456: /** Above this height over the ground an actor is flying, not walking. */
 457: const FLYING_OVER = 0.9;
 458: 
 459: const clamp01 = (v: number) => (v < 0 ? 0 : v > 0.999 ? 0.999 : v);
 460: 
 461: /**
 462:  * The live actor slots, `GRASS_ACTORS` x (x, z, reach, press). Module state
 463:  * and reused every frame: the whole point of this approach is that it
 464:  * allocates nothing and uploads nothing per frame beyond 32 floats.
 465:  *
 466:  * A reach of 0 is an empty slot, so a frame that finds nobody - or a material
 467:  * whose array never binds - leaves the field standing.
 468:  */
 469: const actorSlots = new Float32Array(GRASS_ACTORS * 4);
 470: 
 471: /**
 472:  * The two blades.
 473:  *
 474:  * `simple` is three segments tapering straight to a point, and at rest it is
 475:  * exactly that: a triangle. Cheap, and it is what the lower tiers draw.
 476:  *
 477:  * `detailed` keeps its width most of the way up and tapers late, over six
 478:  * segments, so the vertex shader has something to bend into a curve. The
 479:  * width profile is the half that stops it reading as a cone even before the
 480:  * curve: a blade of grass is a strap that narrows near the tip, not a spike.
 481:  */
 482: const BLADE_ROWS = {
 483:   simple: {
 484:     v: [0, 1 / 3, 2 / 3],
 485:     width: [1.0, 0.78, 0.46],
 486:   },
 487:   detailed: {
 488:     v: [0, 0.2, 0.4, 0.6, 0.78, 0.92],
 489:     width: [1.0, 0.99, 0.94, 0.83, 0.62, 0.33],
 490:   },
 491: } as const;
 492: 
 493: export type BladeKind = keyof typeof BLADE_ROWS;
 494: 
 495: function bladeVertexData(kind: BladeKind): VertexData {
 496:   const { v, width } = BLADE_ROWS[kind];
 497: 
 498:   const positions: number[] = [];
 499:   const uvs: number[] = [];
 500: 
 501:   for (let i = 0; i < v.length; i++) {
 502:     positions.push(-width[i] * 0.5, v[i], 0);
 503:     positions.push(width[i] * 0.5, v[i], 0);
 504:     // u runs -1..1 across the blade; the fragment rounds the normal with it.
 505:     uvs.push(-1, v[i], 1, v[i]);
 506:   }
 507: 
 508:   positions.push(0, 1, 0);
 509:   uvs.push(0, 1);
 510: 
 511:   const indices: number[] = [];
 512: 
 513:   for (let i = 0; i + 1 < v.length; i++) {
 514:     const a = i * 2;
 515:     indices.push(a, a + 1, a + 2, a + 1, a + 3, a + 2);
 516:   }
 517: 
 518:   const last = (v.length - 1) * 2;
 519:   indices.push(last, last + 1, last + 2);
 520: 
 521:   const data = new VertexData();
 522:   data.positions = positions;
 523:   data.uvs = uvs;
 524:   data.indices = indices;
 525: 
 526:   return data;
 527: }
 528: 
 529: function createGrassMaterial(
 530:   scene: Scene,
 531:   cards: GrassCards,
 532:   kind: BladeKind
 533: ): ShaderMaterial {
 534:   const detailed = kind === 'detailed';
 535: 
 536:   const material = new ShaderMaterial(
 537:     'TerrainGrassMaterial',
 538:     scene,
 539:     {
 540:       vertexSource: `
 541:   precision highp float;
 542:   attribute vec3 position;
 543:   attribute vec2 uv;      // x: -1..1 across the blade, y: v along it
 544:   attribute vec4 iRoot; // xyz root in world, w tile slot + placement hash
 545:   attribute vec4 iTint; // rgb the tile's bake, a blade height
 546: 
 547:   uniform mat4 viewProjection;
 548:   uniform mat4 view;
 549:   uniform vec3 cameraPosition;
 550:   uniform float time;
 551:   uniform vec2 grassFade; // x start, y end
 552:   const vec2 windDir = vec2(${GROUND_WIND[0].toFixed(3)}, ${GROUND_WIND[1].toFixed(3)});
 553:   uniform float grassSnow; // settled-snow coverage, 0 = none
 554:   uniform float grassBurnOn; // 0 = nothing is burnt anywhere, skip the fetch
 555:   uniform sampler2D grassRamp;
 556:   uniform sampler2D grassBurn; // the world-space scar, one byte a texel
 557: ${detailed ? `  uniform vec4 grassActors[${GRASS_ACTORS}];` : ''}
 558: 
 559:   varying vec2 vWorldXZ;
 560:   varying vec3 vWorldPos;
 561:   varying float vViewZ;
 562:   varying vec3 vBake;
 563:   varying vec3 vAlbedo;
 564:   varying float vV;
 565:   varying float vBurn; // 0 whole, 1 taken by the fire
 566:   varying float vHeat; // 1 just burnt, 0 once the ember has gone out
 567:   varying float vSeed; // the blade hash, so its dissolve is its own
 568:   varying float vU; // -1..1 across the blade, for its outline
 569: ${detailed ? '  varying vec3 vFace;\n  varying vec3 vSide;' : ''}
 570: 
 571:   void main() {
 572:       float v = position.y;
 573:       int slot = int(iRoot.w);
 574:       float hash = iRoot.w - float(slot);
 575: 
 576:       // Every per-blade quantity is drawn from the one hash, which is itself
 577:       // a pure function of the tile and the blade's index: the field is
 578:       // identical on every load and on every client, which is what lets a
 579:       // block be dropped and rebuilt without the grass moving.
 580:       float yaw = hash * 6.2831853;
 581:       vec2 side = vec2(cos(yaw), sin(yaw));
 582:       // The blade is a flat strap: its width runs along the side vector, so
 583:       // the way it faces - and the way it bends - is across that.
 584:       vec2 facing = vec2(-side.y, side.x);
 585: 
 586:       // The one hash, decorrelated. Cheaper than carrying more per-instance
 587:       // floats and just as unstructured at this scale.
 588:       float h2 = fract(hash * 37.13);
 589:       float h3 = fract(hash * 91.71);
 590: 
 591:       // A blade shrinks to nothing rather than fading: the layer is opaque
 592:       // and an alpha ramp would cost it that.
 593:       float dist = distance(iRoot.xz, cameraPosition.xz);
 594:       float near = 1.0 - smoothstep(grassFade.x, grassFade.y, dist);
 595:       // Snow buries what it settles on, so a blade under full cover keeps
 596:       // only SNOW_BURY of its height.
 597:       float height = iTint.a * near * mix(1.0, ${SNOW_BURY.toFixed(2)}, grassSnow);
 598: 
 599:       // Fire. Read in the *vertex* stage, and that is not a preference: a
 600:       // blade is one to three pixels wide, so a fragment-stage fetch gets
 601:       // derivatives from neighbours that are not on the same blade, lands on
 602:       // the smallest mip and returns garbage. That is the bug that made every
 603:       // blade tip black when the card ramp was first read per fragment.
 604:       //
 605:       // A texel is a quarter tile and the map covers all 256 of them, so the
 606:       // world position is the UV: texel n spans [n, n+1) quarter-tiles and its
 607:       // centre lands exactly on GL's.
 608:       float burnt = grassBurnOn > 0.0
 609:         ? texture2D(grassBurn, iRoot.xz / 256.0).r
 610:         : 0.0;
 611: 
 612:       // This blade's own threshold, from the hash it already carries.
 613:       float burnAt = ${BURN_MIN.toFixed(2)} + h3 * h3 * h3 * ${BURN_SPREAD.toFixed(2)};
 614:       // How far past its own threshold the fire has taken this blade, 0..1.
 615:       // The fragment stage eats the blade with it; the geometry stays whole.
 616:       //
 617:       // It used to scale the height instead, and a blade shortening to nothing
 618:       // is invisible: the field just gets a little sparser, which at any
 619:       // distance reads as nothing at all. What a burning thing looks like is
 620:       // an *edge* - a line of ember travelling across it with the material
 621:       // gone behind it - so that is what this is.
 622:       vBurn = clamp((burnt - burnAt) / ${BURN_WITHER.toFixed(2)}, 0.0, 1.0);
 623:       // The scar decays from 255 over five minutes, so how near the burn is
 624:       // to full *is* how recently the fire went through: the ember cools over
 625:       // the first half minute and the stub is black after that. Without this
 626:       // every stub in the field kept a lit tip for the whole five minutes,
 627:       // which reads as a hillside of candles.
 628:       vHeat = smoothstep(${HEAT_FROM.toFixed(3)}, 1.0, burnt);
 629:       vSeed = hash;
 630:       // How far this one has charred on its way there. A blade blackens before
 631:       // it goes, which is what makes the edge read as fire and not as an
 632:       // eraser passing over the field.
 633:       float charred = smoothstep(0.0, ${CHAR_AT.toFixed(2)}, burnt);
 634: 
 635: ${
 636:   detailed
 637:     ? `
 638:       // Who is standing in this blade, or flying over it. xy world, z reach,
 639:       // w press: positive treads the blade down, negative is a flier's
 640:       // downwash, which shoves it aside without flattening it. z = 0 is an
 641:       // empty slot, so an unbound array leaves the field at rest.
 642:       vec2 shove = vec2(0.0);
 643:       float trodden = 0.0;
 644: 
 645:       for (int i = 0; i < ${GRASS_ACTORS}; i++) {
 646:         vec4 a = grassActors[i];
 647:         if (a.z <= 0.0) continue;
 648: 
 649:         vec2 away = iRoot.xz - a.xy;
 650:         float far = length(away);
 651:         float reach = 1.0 - smoothstep(a.z * ${ACTOR_CORE.toFixed(2)}, a.z, far);
 652:         if (reach <= 0.0) continue;
 653: 
 654:         shove += (far > 0.0001 ? away / far : vec2(1.0, 0.0)) * reach * abs(a.w);
 655: 
 656:         // The deepest press over this blade, not the sum of them. A blade
 657:         // already flat cannot be flattened again by a second foot, and summing
 658:         // said it could: along a trail the crumbs are stamped STAMP_STEP
 659:         // apart and their falloffs overlap between the footfalls but not on
 660:         // them, so the *gaps* summed past the clamp while the footfalls
 661:         // themselves sat below it. The trail came out scalloped - flatter
 662:         // between the steps than under them - and, worse, each scallop fell
 663:         // off the clamp at its own moment as the presses recovered, so the
 664:         // trail lifted in chunks rather than rising.
 665:         //
 666:         // Sampled across a real trail: 14 of 38 points pinned at the clamp and
 667:         // 2.68 of total variation along it, against 0.72 taking the max, which
 668:         // decays monotonically and never clamps at all.
 669:         //
 670:         // shove above stays a sum: it is a direction, neighbours pointing
 671:         // opposite ways cancel rather than pile up, and nothing clamps it.
 672:         trodden = max(trodden, reach * max(a.w, 0.0));
 673:       }
 674: 
 675:       height *= 1.0 - min(trodden, 1.0) * ${TRAMPLE_FLATTEN.toFixed(2)};`
 676:     : ''
 677: }
 678: 
 679:       // The wave. One phase for the whole field, travelling along the wind,
 680:       // so neighbours crest together and the eye has something to follow.
 681:       // No per-blade term here on purpose - that is what made it jitter.
 682:       float along = dot(iRoot.xz, windDir);
 683:       float wave = sin(time * ${WAVE_RATE.toFixed(2)} - along * ${WAVE_FREQ.toFixed(2)});
 684:       float chop = sin(time * ${CHOP_RATE.toFixed(2)} - along * ${CHOP_FREQ.toFixed(2)} + yaw);
 685: 
 686:       // Biased so the field leans downwind on the whole and the wave rides on
 687:       // top, rather than swinging symmetrically through upright.
 688:       float gust = ${WIND_BIAS.toFixed(2)}
 689:         + wave * ${WAVE_BEND.toFixed(3)}
 690:         + chop * ${CHOP_BEND.toFixed(3)};
 691: 
 692:       // Weighted v*v so the root stays planted and the tip does the travelling.
 693:       float bend = gust * v * v * height;
 694: 
 695:       vec3 world = iRoot.xyz;
 696:       world.xz += side * (position.x * ${BLADE_WIDTH.toFixed(4)} * mix(0.75, 1.3, h3));
 697:       world.y += v * height;
 698:       world.xz += windDir * bend;
 699: ${
 700:   detailed
 701:     ? `
 702:       // The arc. A blade at rest is not a spike standing straight up - it
 703:       // leans and curves over, and how far is the single thing that stops a
 704:       // field reading as a bed of nails. v^1.6 rather than v^2 so the curve
 705:       // starts low on the blade instead of only kinking at the tip.
 706:       float arc = mix(${ARC_MIN.toFixed(2)}, ${ARC_MAX.toFixed(2)}, h2);
 707:       float curve = arc * pow(v, 1.6);
 708:       world.xz += facing * (curve * height);
 709:       // What the blade spends going sideways it does not spend going up.
 710:       world.y -= curve * curve * height * ${ARC_DROOP.toFixed(2)};
